{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^#|''^##|''^###|Open questions for the owner|Contradictions found|''^Q[1-7]|Q1|Q2|Q3|Q4|Q5|Q6|Q7'\"' collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md\"","aggregated_output":"4:# dao.stream.serve — Serving Any Stream Over a Network\n8:## 1. The problem stated in contract terms\n23:## 2. Layering\n47:## 3. The serve protocol\n49:### 3.1 Sessions\n55:### 3.2 Frames\n89:### 3.3 Mechanical derivation\n107:### 3.4 Idempotence, loss and duplication\n119:### 3.5 Batching\n123:### 3.6 Anchors across a hop\n129:### 3.7 Held reads (optional, v1 decision needed)\n131:Across a network a `blocked` answer costs a round trip. A `next` request may carry `:serve/hold true`; a server that honors it records the demand in session state and, on each of its own driver steps, re-polls the source, answering when the outcome is no longer `blocked` or when its hold budget (a constructor policy in the driver's clock domain) elapses, in which case it answers the `blocked` it has. No operation waits: the session holds state, the step returns. A proxy must tolerate a server that ignores the flag. Whether v1 includes this is an owner decision (Q2).\n133:## 4. The proxy handle: declared nature\n147:The proxy's writer surface is on the **session's ordered outbound path**, as ws's is on the connection's; the source's answer to an append arrives on the proxy's event medium as `{:serve/event :serve/appended :dao.stream.serve/id n :serve/outcome <map>}`. This is the contract's Writing rule and the ws precedent, and it has a cost for the UCF (Q1).\n156:## 5. The server\n166:## 6. Channels\n168:### 6.1 Channel contract\n179:### 6.2 WebSocket channel\n188:### 6.3 UDP channel\n199:### 6.4 A channel over a channel\n203:## 7. Reachability\n205:### 7.1 The serve descriptor\n220:### 7.2 Direct\n224:### 7.3 Relay: outbound-only serving\n230:### 7.4 Rendezvous and UDP hole punching\n236:### 7.5 P2P symmetry\n240:### 7.6 Liveness\n244:## 8. Host isolation\n258:## 9. UCF integration\n264:- **Lifetime.** The exporter must keep the entry served while the migrated task may need it. The correct home for that obligation is a lease (`dao.lease.md`): the served entry is a grant, the resumer renews, and lapse unserves. Until that lease exists, the exporter keeps the entry until its own composition retires it, and a resumer that arrives after retirement gets `not-found`. This is stated as an open question (Q6) rather than designed here.\n267:## 10. Invariant compliance\n276:## 11. Concurrency and linearization\n280:## 12. What is contract, transport-owned and deferred\n286:## 13. Completion criteria\n298:## 14. Deferred\n304:# Open questions for the owner\n306:- **Q1. Append through a proxy.** As designed, `append!` on a proxy answers `ok` for acceptance into the session's outbound path and the source's outcome arrives later on the event medium, as ws does and the contract's Writing section allows. The UCF's `:put` rule (\"retries its retained value and resumes on `ok`\") would then resume a program on an append the source may still refuse. Alternative: the proxy answers `full` until the source's outcome arrives and then reports that outcome, holding the value meanwhile, which gives the writer the source's truth from `append!` itself at the cost of a proxy-level convention (one held value, writer retries the same value). Recommendation: keep the contract-consistent design and amend UCF §7.4.3 so that a `:put` on a served stream resumes when the source's outcome is observed, not on outbound `ok`.\n307:- **Q2. Held reads in v1.** They cut network chatter for `blocked`-heavy readers such as a parked VM polling a response cell, at the cost of per-session server state and a hold budget. Recommendation: include, since the UCF's dominant pattern is exactly a kept response cursor polled until a value lands.\n308:- **Q3. Peer identity.** Opaque composition-assigned strings now, self-certifying kickoff-hash identities later, or self-certifying from the start. Recommendation: opaque now; the relay routes by string equality either way, and signature verification is an additive rule on `:serve/register`.\n309:- **Q4. Relay and rendezvous gating.** v1 accepts any `:serve/register` and `:serve/dial`. Recommendation: accept for the dev-only repo, record postage as the intended answer.\n310:- **Q5. Accept OD-1, OD-2 and OD-3 (a)+(2) now.** This design is not implementable without OD-3 and is dishonest without OD-2; OD-1 is needed only for the `:establishing` answer. Recommendation: accept all three as a precondition of the implementation phase.\n311:- **Q6. Lifetime of a served entry after migration.** Lease-governed (recommended, via `dao.lease`), or composition-retired with `not-found` as the honest late answer.\n312:- **Q7. Naming.** `dao.stream.serve` for this layer and a rename of today's `dao.stream.serving` to `dao.stream.serving.copy` or similar, so the two models are not confused.\n314:# Contradictions found\n322:| medium | `docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:554-556` | \"a `:put` wait retries its retained value and resumes with it on `ok`\" | Through a proxy, `ok` is outbound acceptance. Resume on the observed source outcome (Q1), or adopt the alternative and say so. |\n327:| low | `src/cljc/dao/stream/serving.cljc:1-11, 234-237` | \"Host-owned WebSocket serving composition\"; forwards from `:forward-anchor` (default oldest) into every accepted socket | Not wrong, but it is copy-serving. Rename or re-describe (Q7) so that \"serving\" does not imply attachment to the original stream. |\n328:| low | `docs/design/dao.lease.md:286-289` | \"A remote holder learns of a reclaim by observing … an ordinary `:ws/closed`. No close code is assigned here\" | With serve sessions, reclaim of a served entry is `:serve/close` with `:serve/reason`, independent of the channel's close codes; update once Q6 is decided. |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^#|''^##|''^###|7'\"\\\\.4\\\\.3|7\\\\.5\\\\.3|invariant|identity|outcome|cursor|gap' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\"","aggregated_output":"1:## §7. The Universal Continuation Format (Proposed)\n8:To safely \"lift on park\" and \"lower on resume\" across heterogeneous boundaries without violating host isolation or concurrency invariants, the following contracts must be fully specified before this feature is accepted:\n18:# Universal Continuation Format — protocol draft\n29:§7.3–§7.7, one section each, in dependency order: code identity first, because\n37:## §7.1 Stance: a continuation is a value, not a session\n65:(sharing thresholds differ, §7.5.3), so the encodings address differently,\n66:while remaining one *checkpoint occurrence*. Content identity is for\n67:deduplication and materialization; occurrence identity is what custody names\n76:## §7.2 Vocabulary and envelope\n94: :yin.k/arbitration {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}     ; §7.7.3  the authority medium, named in the value\n96: :yin.k/values      {…}                        ; §7.5.3  shared subvalues, by content address\n97: :yin.k/cells       {…}                        ; §7.5.3  cursor cells: logical identity + position\n122:Both envelope kinds take their identity by the same rule: `:yin.k/id` is the\n127:stream outcome is involved, it is preserved unchanged under its own dispatch\n128:key `:dao.stream/outcome` — UCF never renames or nests it under a private\n131:## §7.3 Code identity — content-addressed semantic profiles (blocker 5)\n133:### 7.3.1 The problem restated as data\n139:### 7.3.2 The canonical instruction vector and its address\n148:but carried datom vocabulary into the identity layer — attribute names and\n198:  decision. The identity equivalence claim above is conditional on this\n246:### 7.3.3 The contract stamp\n251:opcode table — leaving reso