{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.lease.md | sed -n '70,110p;202,250p;280,295p'\"","aggregated_output":"    70\t`:released` or `:lapsed` for one lease; or carries a reading older than one\n    71\talready observed. A judge reading a defective fact has read no lease fact.\n    72\t\n    73\t## Authority\n    74\t\n    75\t- **Only grantor-authored facts establish terms.** A holder cannot grant itself\n    76\t  tenure. A holder fact establishes no term; a delayed holder renewal is still\n    77\t  evidence and counts as such.\n    78\t- **A renewal counts only when its attributed author is the lease's holder.**\n    79\t  A renewal from any other author is a fact about that author, and silence is\n    80\t  measured as though it had not arrived.\n    81\t- **At most one of `:accepted` or `:rejected` answers a proposal.**\n    82\t  Renegotiating is a new proposal with a new proposal identity.\n    83\t- **A proposal creates no state.** Nothing exists until `:accepted`. A grantor\n    84\t  owes no answer, and a holder's wait on an unanswered proposal is bounded by\n    85\t  nothing in this vocabulary.\n    86\t- **A grantor may grant unsolicited**, with no proposal, in which case the\n    87\t  holder is the party the grant is delivered to.\n    88\t- **A `:policy` cause may not consist of declining to count evidence\n    89\t  received.** Eligible renewals are counted whether or not another condition\n    90\t  ends the lease.\n    91\t\n    92\t## Prohibitions\n    93\t\n    94\t- A lease may not extend retention, defer eviction, or gate history, and no\n    95\t  DaoStream operation may consult a lease.\n    96\t- No timer holds a callback and no notification marks a lapse.\n    97\t- No absolute time appears in any fact.\n    98\t- A reclaim frees the resource, never the record.\n    99\t- A holder renews from its own control flow. No machinery renews on its behalf.\n   100\t\n   101\t## Time\n   102\t\n   103\tTime reaches lease code only as data; neither judge nor holder reads a host\n   104\tclock. Both read a tick stream their composition wires. Readings on one stream\n   105\tnever decrease, and a composition persisting a ledger across a restart keeps\n   106\tits readings comparable across it.\n   107\t\n   108\tA tick-stream gap makes a pass late, not wrong; a lease-fact gap is governed\n   109\tunder *Judging with incomplete evidence*.\n   110\t\n   202\t## The holder\n   203\t\n   204\t- **Observe the grant before acting**, and establish that the grantor authored\n   205\t  it by the same attribution its composition supplies to the judge. A holder\n   206\t  that has not observed its grant holds nothing.\n   207\t- **Renew at less than half the duration**, measured against ticks on its own\n   208\t  tick stream. A renewal is an append returning `:dao.stream/ok`; any other\n   209\t  outcome does not advance the holder's bound.\n   210\t- **Stop acting at the bound** — the earlier of the duration since the later of\n   211\t  its last renewal and its observed grant, and the cap the grant carries —\n   212\t  whether or not anything has been heard.\n   213\t- **Release when done.** The grantor still performs the reclaim and records\n   214\t  it.\n   215\t- **Treat an unusable reading as unanswerable.** A reading that is not a\n   216\t  single-entry `{unit, positive integer magnitude}` within the composition's\n   217\t  table answers nothing: every holder discipline fails closed on it, and a\n   218\t  flow that guards on its bound alone does not act.\n   219\t\n   220\tFor cap purposes, the holder measures the cap interval from the reading at\n   221\twhich it observed the grant; that reading may be later than the grantor\n   222\tjudge's tenure start. The holder's observation-flight interval does not\n   223\textend, delay, or alter the judge's cap or reclaim decision; a resource\n   224\trequiring exclusion must enforce it through fencing.\n   225\t\n   226\tThe bound bounds attention, not access; a holder needing exclusion obtains it\n   227\tfrom the resource.\n   228\t\n   229\t## Composition duties\n   230\t\n   231\tA composition that grants leases owes all of the following.\n   232\t\n   233\t- **A judging interpreter, and a runtime driving it at a declared cadence** —\n   234\t  the maximum interval between *completed* passes.\n   235\t- **A tick stream** for the judge, and one for every holder.\n   236\t- **A tolerance** supplied to the judge, possibly zero.\n   237\t- **An attribution resolver** for the judge and for every holder observing a\n   238\t  grant — its own answer to who authored a fact, by per-author media, an\n   239\t  envelope key, or a transport's attachment identity, since DaoStream supplies\n   240\t  none. One handed no resolver, or one incompatible with its medium, is refused\n   241\t  at assembly.\n   242\t- **A reclaim procedure per subject**, idempotent, reporting success.\n   243\t- **A stream the grantor writes grants and `:lapsed` to**, and the medium each\n   244\t  recipient reads its carried facts from.\n   245\t- **Media that either retain without eviction or declare evict-oldest**,\n   246\t  refused at assembly otherwise.\n   247\t- **For durable resources, three things this vocabulary does not supply**: a\n   248\t  judge whose durability matches the resource, a rule for which incarnation may\n   249\t  reclaim, and fencing from the resource. A composition that has not settled\n   250\t  all three keeps its leases process-scoped.\n   280\t## Carriage\n   281\t\n   282\tProposals, grants, refusals, releases and renewals cross a boundary as ordinary\n   283\tpayload, carried by the composition to the medium the recipient reads. Carriage\n   284\tis delivery, not a second authoring.\n   285\t\n   286\t`:lapsed` does not cross; it is the grantor's record on the grantor's stream. A\n   287\tremote holder learns of a reclaim by observing it — for a served connection, an\n   288\tordinary `:ws/closed`. No close code is assigned here; distinguishing reclaim on\n   289\tthe wire belongs to `dao.stream.ws.md`'s deferred close-code design.\n   290\t\n   291\t## Out of scope\n   292\t\n   293\t- **Delegated renewal.** A renewal is evidence about its author.\n   294\t- **Transfer of a lease between holders.**\n   295\t- **Who may be granted anything at all**, and **what is leasable** — this\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '1,195p;200,355p;535,585p;805,935p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# DaoStream Design\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: agreed design target. This document is the DaoStream contract. It does\n     4\tnot redesign Yin.VM. Yin.VM is a downstream consumer and will be addressed only\n     5\tafter this contract is implemented and tested.\n     6\t\n     7\t## Purpose\n     8\t\n     9\tThis contract derives from the foundational axioms and non-negotiable\n    10\tinvariants of [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md). Two axioms bear on it\n    11\tdirectly, named here once so the sections below can cite rather than restate\n    12\tthem:\n    13\t\n    14\t1. **Everything is a Stream** — all IO and data flow through append-only\n    15\t   streams.\n    16\t2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics** — data is syntax; semantics emerge\n    17\t   only through interpretation. One truth, many perspectives.\n    18\t\n    19\tDaoStream is the passive substrate for IO in datom.world — passive because\n    20\tinterpretation and execution must not collapse into one layer. Axiom 1 is\n    21\twhat makes this the layer every host boundary crosses. A stream carries\n    22\tvalues and decides nothing about them:\n    23\t\n    24\t- **What the values mean** is not its business — Axiom 2. An element\n    25\t  judged, filtered, or typed at this layer destroys the perspectives nobody\n    26\t  has taken yet.\n    27\t- **Which interpreter observes them** is not recorded — a registry of who\n    28\t  is listening would be hidden global state.\n    29\t- **When an interpreter runs** is not its decision — \"do not introduce\n    30\t  callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream\", seen from the stream's\n    31\t  side: DaoStream invokes nothing, so control is never inverted and\n    32\t  causality stays explicit (see The IO Model).\n    33\t\n    34\tThe Invariants below are these denials in operational form.\n    35\t\n    36\tThe design separates five concepts:\n    37\t\n    38\t1. A **creation specification** is data for creating a new logical stream.\n    39\t2. A **handle** is a host-local operational stream value — the\n    40\t   implementation of the `dao.stream` protocols.\n    41\t3. A **logical-stream identity** is transport-independent plain data shared by\n    42\t   every handle and descriptor for one stream.\n    43\t4. A **portable descriptor** is reachability data used to attach to an existing\n    44\t   stream; it includes the logical-stream identity but is not that identity.\n    45\t5. A **cursor** is immutable interpreter-owned observation state for one\n    46\t   logical stream.\n    47\t\n    48\tCreation is not attachment. A descriptor is not a creation specification or an\n    49\tidentity. A handle is not portable identity. A cursor is not handle-owned\n    50\tstate.\n    51\t\n    52\tSeven operations range over them, each returning an outcome map (see\n    53\tResult Convention):\n    54\t\n    55\t| Operation    | Consumes               | Yields             |\n    56\t|--------------|------------------------|--------------------|\n    57\t| `create!`    | creation specification | handle outcome     |\n    58\t| `attach!`    | portable descriptor    | handle outcome     |\n    59\t| `descriptor` | handle                 | descriptor outcome |\n    60\t| `cursor`     | handle, anchor         | cursor outcome     |\n    61\t| `next`       | handle, cursor         | read outcome       |\n    62\t| `append!`    | handle, value          | write outcome      |\n    63\t| `close!`     | handle                 | close outcome      |\n    64\t\n    65\tA handle implements the subset of the **handle surface** its transport\n    66\tdeclares (see Surfaces). `create!` and `attach!` are not part of it: they\n    67\tconsume no handle and produce one.\n    68\t\n    69\t## Invariants\n    70\t\n    71\t- Stream elements are data. DaoStream assigns them no intrinsic meaning.\n    72\t- Reading is non-destructive. A reader advances its own cursor, not the stream.\n    73\t- Multiple cursors may observe the same retained value independently.\n    74\t- Interpreters own cursors and decide when to call `next`.\n    75\t- A runtime may drive an interpreter, but DaoStream does not require a runtime.\n    76\t- Closing a stream changes future availability but does not itself erase\n    77\t  retained history.\n    78\t- Retention may be bounded. A cursor over evicted history receives a gap\n    79\t  outcome as data; the reader decides what missing data means.\n    80\t- All expected operational outcomes are data, not exceptions or callbacks.\n    81\t- No operation waits. Every operation returns what is true at the moment it is\n    82\t  called; an answer not yet knowable arrives later as data.\n    83\t- No hidden global state. DaoStream keeps no registry of streams, handles, or\n    84\t  observers, and no operation consults ambient state.\n    85\t- A logical stream has an identity that is plain data, the same through every\n    86\t  handle on it, stable across attachment lifecycles and across serialization.\n    87\t  Projecting that identity never fails.\n    88\t\n    89\tEvery clause below must be consistent with these invariants, and most derive\n    90\tfrom one directly. Where a clause is instead an interoperability decision — the\n    91\tdescriptor's properties, the result keyword namespace, the anchor set — it is\n    92\tstated as a decision rather than dressed as a deduction. A proposed addition\n    93\tthat contradicts an invariant does not belong in this contract; one that merely\n    94\tcannot be deduced from them must say which decision it is and why.\n    95\t\n    96\t## Result Convention\n    97\t\n    98\tEvery DaoStream operation returns data.\n    99\t\n   100\t- Each operation's set of outcomes is **exhaustive**: a transport may produce\n   101\t  fewer of them, never any outside the set. Which subset it produces is part of\n   102\t  its declared nature (see Surfaces). An outcome is a map whose\n   103\t  `:dao.stream/outcome` key names the outcome with a qualified keyword. Every\n   104\t  operation returns such a map — including projections like `descriptor` and\n   105\t  `cursor`, whose success map carries the projected value.\n   106\t- `:dao.stream/outcome` is present in every result map and is never repeated\n   107\t  in the per-outcome tables below; the keys those tables name are required in\n   108\t  addition to it.\n   109\t- Result maps are **open**: the contract fixes which keys are required per\n   110\t  outcome, and a consumer must ignore keys it does not understand. This is\n   111\t  what allows future extensions (for example readiness notification) to be\n   112\t  added without breaking existing consumers.\n   113\t- Exceptions are reserved for defects in the host's own assembly of\n   114\t  DaoStream, detected before any operation runs. Everything observable at an\n   115\t  operation returns data: closed, full, blocked, end, gap, not found,\n   116\t  malformed input, and transport defects are all outcomes, never exceptions\n   117\t  and never callbacks.\n   118\t- All qualified keywords in results live under `:dao.stream/…`.\n   119\t\n   120\t## The IO Model\n   121\t\n   122\tThree models are available for expressing IO. DaoStream is deliberately the\n   123\tthird.\n   124\t\n   125\t**Blocking (synchronous) IO.** The operation parks until it has an answer,\n   126\tthen returns it. Control flow stays sequential and the call site reads as\n   127\tordinary code, which is the whole of its appeal. The cost is that something\n   128\tmust wait — a thread per pending operation, held for its duration — and it\n   129\trequires a host that can park at all.\n   130\t\n   131\t**Callback (asynchronous) IO.** The operation registers a continuation and\n   132\treturns at once; the runtime invokes the continuation when the answer\n   133\tarrives. Nothing waits, so one thread carries many pending operations: this\n   134\tis the model that scales. Its cost is inverted control. The caller no longer\n   135\tdecides when it observes the answer, and the inversion is viral — on a host\n   136\twithout blocking, a promise cannot be absorbed inside a function, because\n   137\tawaiting returns to the caller at the first suspension point and makes the\n   138\tcaller's own result a promise in turn. Whatever is reached from a callback\n   139\tis reached from a callback.\n   140\t\n   141\t**Non-blocking polling.** The operation returns immediately with whatever is\n   142\ttrue now, including the answer \"nothing yet, ask again.\" Nothing parks and\n   143\tnothing is inverted: the caller keeps control and decides when to ask again.\n   144\tThis is POSIX `O_NONBLOCK` without `select`, and it is DaoStream's model.\n   145\t\n   146\t`next` is total and non-blocking (see Reading): every state of the stream\n   147\thas a defined data answer, and `:dao.stream/blocked` is `EAGAIN` — this\n   148\tposition holds nothing yet. `append!`'s `:dao.stream/full` is the same\n   149\tanswer for writes. No operation in this contract parks, and none takes a\n   150\tcallback; the Result Convention's \"never exceptions and never callbacks\" is\n   151\tthis model stated as a rule about results.\n   152\t\n   153\tThe rule generalizes past `next`: **no operation waits.** An operation that\n   154\tcannot yet know its answer returns the outcome true at the moment it is\n   155\tcalled, and the eventual answer arrives the way every other fact does — as\n   156\tdata, observed later, through an ordinary operation or on a stream. A\n   157\tDaoStream operation is never a request for something to happen and be\n   158\treported back. It is a question about now.\n   159\t\n   160\t### Why this model and not the other two\n   161\t\n   162\tThe hosts do not offer the same choices. The JVM has both blocking and\n   163\tasynchronous IO. JavaScript and Dart have only the asynchronous kind: there\n   164\tis no blocking read to call and no way to wait for one, because the single\n   165\tevent loop that would deliver the answer is the one a waiting call would be\n   166\toccupying — waiting deadlocks rather than merely costing a thread.\n   167\t\n   168\tA blocking contract is therefore unimplementable on most of the targets. A\n   169\tcallback contract is implementable on all of them and inverts control on all\n   170\tof them, including the host that never needed it. Non-blocking polling is\n   171\tthe only one of the three that means the same thing everywhere — the same\n   172\toperations, the same outcome sets, the same control flow — which is what\n   173\tlets one surface serve clj, cljs, and cljd without the shape of the API\n   174\tdiffering by host.\n   175\t\n   176\t### What it costs\n   177\t\n   178\tPolling without readiness notification is O(n) in streams observed: a reader\n   179\tholding ten thousand idle streams must ask all ten thousand to learn which\n   180\thave data. This is `select` without `select`, and it is a real limit rather\n   181\tthan an oversight — the extension that answers it is reserved below. The\n   182\tcost also lands where it can be paid: DaoStream schedules nothing, so\n   183\tcadence belongs to the runtime driving the interpreters, and a readiness\n   184\tmechanism belongs there too.\n   185\t\n   186\t### The readiness extension\n   187\t\n   188\tThis contract has no readiness or waiter extension. A reader that receives\n   189\t`:dao.stream/blocked` retries `next` when it chooses; retry cadence is the\n   190\tconcern of the interpreter or the runtime driving it, not of the stream.\n   191\t\n   192\tA future readiness extension would be additive: an optional protocol on\n   193\ttransports that support it, plus optional keys in operation results (for\n   194\texample a wake token list on `append!`), which the open-map rule already\n   195\tpermits. Nothing in this contract needs to change for that; no consumer of\n   200\trounds, and the contract remains untouched by it.\n   201\t\n   202\t## Creation and Attachment\n   203\t\n   204\tCreation brings a new logical stream into existence. Attachment joins an\n   205\texisting one. They are separate operations with separate inputs.\n   206\t\n   207\t- `create!` consumes a creation specification and returns a handle on a\n   208\t  new logical stream. `descriptor` on that handle returns `{ok}` with both\n   209\t  `:dao.stream/descriptor`, the transport-specific reachability value, and\n   210\t  `:dao.stream/identity`, the transport-independent logical-stream identity.\n   211\t  The descriptor carries the same identity internally; the sibling projection\n   212\t  and its value inside `:dao.stream/descriptor` must be structurally equal.\n   213\t  Both projections are total and outlive any attachment, so `descriptor` has\n   214\t  no failure outcome.\n   215\t- `attach!` consumes a portable descriptor and returns a handle on an\n   216\t  attachment to the **same** logical stream the descriptor names. Attaching\n   217\t  never creates.\n   218\t- A creation specification says how to build a stream. A descriptor only\n   219\t  names one — never executable code, never a construction program, never a\n   220\t  request to load a plugin.\n   221\t\n   222\tStreams are values that can be sent through streams — up to a serialization\n   223\tboundary. An in-memory stream whose admission declaration permits host\n   224\treferences can carry a handle intact. A dynamically delivered handle travels\n   225\tas composition data together with its declared surface, for example\n   226\t`{:dao.stream/handle h :dao.stream/surface #{:reader :writer}}`; the receiving\n   227\tinterpreter uses only that declared surface, and attaching to what it already\n   228\tholds is meaningless. A composition may omit the wrapper only when the\n   229\treceiver's fixed contract already declares the surface. But a handle does not\n   230\tsurvive encoding, and any\n   231\ttransport that encodes its elements — a file as much as a network socket —\n   232\tis a serialization boundary. Descriptors exist for crossing those\n   233\tboundaries, where the live value cannot travel and a name must. (What a\n   234\ttransport can carry is transport-owned: handing an encoding transport a\n   235\tvalue it cannot encode is reported as data like any other outcome.)\n   236\t`attach!` therefore consumes only descriptors, and\n   237\tresolves them against the transport's inherent operational state — the\n   238\tstreams a network endpoint serves because serving them is what it is, the\n   239\tfiles a filesystem holds. There is no stream table in this contract: no\n   240\tregistry maps descriptors to live handles, so a descriptor arriving\n   241\tsomewhere with nothing behind it — a ring buffer's descriptor on any other\n   242\thost, or on its own host absent a directory some composition chose to keep —\n   243\thonestly resolves to `:dao.stream/not-found`.\n   244\t\n   245\t### Envelopes\n   246\t\n   247\tA **creation specification** is a map with one required key:\n   248\t\n   249\t- `:dao.stream/type` — a qualified keyword naming the transport. This is the\n   250\t  dispatch key.\n   251\t\n   252\tEvery other key is transport-owned, qualified under the transport's namespace\n   253\t(for example a ring buffer's capacity), and validated by the selected handler\n   254\t— generic validation checks only the envelope, and a handler that rejects its\n   255\town keys returns `:dao.stream/invalid-spec`.\n   256\t\n   257\tThe **portable descriptor** is inspired by CORBA's interoperable object\n   258\treference. It is reachability data, not the logical-stream identity itself.\n   259\tIts properties are settled:\n   260\t\n   261\t- It carries `:dao.stream/type` — the transport, as above — as its dispatch\n   262\t  key.\n   263\t- It carries the logical-stream identity under the contract-owned\n   264\t  `:dao.stream/identity` key. Different descriptors may reach\n   265\t  the same logical stream through different endpoints; their reachability data\n   266\t  may differ, but their logical-stream identity is structurally equal. The same\n   267\t  descriptor attaches to the same stream every time.\n   268\t- It is self-contained. If the stream is remote, it contains whatever is\n   269\t  needed to connect — perhaps an IP address and port. The entry data is\n   270\t  transport-owned; a handler that cannot make sense of it returns\n   271\t  `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor`.\n   272\t- A transport whose attachments are distinguishable assigns each attachment\n   273\t  an opaque, structurally serializable value, unique among the attachments\n   274\t  that transport can tell apart. Every attachment handle the transport mints\n   275\t  has such an identity, whether the handle is returned by `attach!` or\n   276\t  minted from a host connection accepted without an `attach!` call. An\n   277\t  `attach!` success map carries the value under `:dao.stream/attachment`.\n   278\t  When no `attach!` call produces the handle, the transport specification\n   279\t  names the deposited event or other stream-native mechanism by which the\n   280\t  composition receives the identity. Wherever an answer or event for that\n   281\t  attachment is displaced onto another channel, the same value correlates it\n   282\t  there. The key and correlation rule are the contract's; minting,\n   283\t  representation, and the server-side delivery mechanism are the\n   284\t  transport's. Transports that cannot distinguish attachments omit it.\n   285\t- It carries no authorization. Nothing in this contract gates who may attach:\n   286\t  a descriptor is a name, and a host that can reach what it names attaches.\n   287\t  Whether attachment should be gated at all, and by what, is a question this\n   288\t  contract does not answer and does not reserve room for — an outcome or key\n   289\t  held open for an undesigned mechanism constrains that mechanism's design\n   290\t  from a document with no business specifying it. Should gating arrive, it\n   291\t  arrives as an addition, which the open-map rule and this section's own\n   292\t  transport-owned keys already permit.\n   293\t\n   294\tFor the time being, the operative guarantee is the round trip: `descriptor`\n   295\ton a live handle returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/descriptor`,\n   296\ta value that can be sent on a stream and reconstructed on the other end, and\n   297\t`:dao.stream/identity`, equal through every handle and descriptor for the\n   298\tlogical stream,\n   299\twhere `attach!` on the reconstructed value attaches to the stream it names —\n   300\tprovided the receiving host can reach what it names at all (see Creation and\n   301\tAttachment on reachability), and allowing that a transport which cannot know\n   302\tthat at call time reports it later as data.\n   303\t\n   304\tBoth envelopes are plain data through and through: every value must survive\n   305\tthe host serialization codec structurally unchanged, so equality is\n   306\tstructural, and no key holds a function, a host object, a live handle, or\n   307\tidentity smuggled in metadata. Like every DaoStream map, envelopes are open —\n   308\ta consumer ignores qualified keys it does not understand.\n   309\t\n   310\t`create!` outcomes:\n   311\t\n   312\t| Outcome                       | Meaning                                       | Required keys        |\n   313\t|-------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|----------------------|\n   314\t| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A new logical stream exists, its identity settled at once. Implies not that every resource backing it has finished being acquired. | `:dao.stream/handle` |\n   315\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-spec`    | The creation specification is malformed.      | —                    |\n   316\t| `:dao.stream/not-found`       | No transport here matches `:dao.stream/type`. | —                    |\n   317\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The handler failed.                           | —                    |\n   318\t\n   319\t`attach!` outcomes:\n   320\t\n   321\t| Outcome                          | Meaning                                                                             | Required keys        |\n   322\t|----------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------|\n   323\t| `:dao.stream/ok`                 | A handle exists through which this attachment is operated. Implies neither that a far end has confirmed the stream it names nor that establishment has completed. Where either answer is one only the far end can give, it arrives later as data, on the channel the transport names (see Surfaces). | `:dao.stream/handle`, and `:dao.stream/attachment` where the transport distinguishes attachments |\n   324\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor` | The descriptor is malformed.                                                        | —                    |\n   325\t| `:dao.stream/not-found`          | The descriptor names no stream here, or no transport matches its type.              | —                    |\n   326\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error`    | The handler failed.                                                                 | —                    |\n   327\t\n   328\tWhere a transport acquires its medium through a process rather than at once —\n   329\tbinding a port, opening a handle through an API that only answers later — `ok`\n   330\tstill means the logical stream exists: its identity is minted, its sequence is\n   331\tempty, and the handle is operable. What it does not mean is that the stream can\n   332\tyet accept values. The handle reports that through its own operations, exactly\n   333\tas any other transient condition is reported, and the result of the acquisition\n   334\tarrives as data.\n   335\t\n   336\tThis is permitted only where logical existence is genuinely independent of the\n   337\tresource. A transport that cannot mint identity and lifecycle before its\n   338\tacquisition completes is not creating a stream and deferring its medium; it is\n   339\tdeferring the creation itself, and `ok` would be false. Such a transport does\n   340\tnot implement `create!` — what it needs is a request whose result arrives\n   341\tlater, which is an ordinary interpreter over streams, not an operation here.\n   342\t\n   343\tCreation defers differently from attachment, and the difference is why this is\n   344\ta caveat rather than a new outcome. `attach!` defers a **remote confirmation**:\n   345\tan answer only a far end can give, which is why it arrives as deposited data.\n   346\t`create!` has no far end. What it defers is **local acquisition**, whose\n   347\tfailure surfaces through the handle the caller already holds. A \"not yet\"\n   348\toutcome would have to return no handle — leaving the caller an identity it\n   349\tcannot operate, and nothing to retry against.\n   350\t\n   351\t### Host Dispatch\n   352\t\n   353\tWhen a creation specification or portable descriptor arrives dynamically —\n   354\tfor example off a stream — the host looks at it and dispatches on\n   355\t`:dao.stream/type`. The type resolves to a map that has the transport's\n   535\t\n   536\t## Reading\n   537\t\n   538\t`next` takes a handle and a cursor and returns an outcome immediately.\n   539\tIt is total and non-blocking: every state of the stream has a defined data\n   540\tanswer.\n   541\t\n   542\t| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                       | Required keys                                                                           |\n   543\t|-------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n   544\t| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A value was observed.                                                         | `:dao.stream/value`, `:dao.stream/cursor` (successor)                                   |\n   545\t| `:dao.stream/blocked`         | Nothing at this position yet; retry later.                                    | —                                                                                       |\n   546\t| `:dao.stream/end`             | What this handle is on is closed and this position is past the retained tail. | —                                                                                       |\n   547\t| `:dao.stream/gap`             | This position was evicted.                                                    | `:dao.stream/cursor` (earliest retained position, or the tail when nothing is retained) |\n   548\t| `:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch` | The cursor was minted by a different logical stream.                          | —                                                                                       |\n   549\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-cursor`  | The value is not a cursor.                                                    | —                                                                                       |\n   550\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the read; nothing was observed.               | —                                                                                       |\n   551\t\n   552\tReading never mutates the stream. Two readers with cursors at the same\n   553\tposition observe the same retained value; neither can starve or affect the\n   554\tother. There is no destructive read anywhere in the contract — no drain, no\n   555\ttake. An operation that removes an element as it reads would make one\n   556\treader's progress every other reader's data loss.\n   557\t\n   558\t## Writing\n   559\t\n   560\t`append!` takes a handle and a value and returns an outcome:\n   561\t\n   562\t| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                                                                                              | Required keys |\n   563\t|-------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------|\n   564\t| `:dao.stream/ok`              | The value is accepted at the next position in the sequence this handle's writer surface is on. Implies neither local readability nor remote delivery. | —             |\n   565\t| `:dao.stream/full`            | The transport does not accept the value; nothing was appended. Whether this is transient or permanent is the transport's declared nature (see Retention and Gaps). | —             |\n   566\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-value`   | The transport cannot carry this value (for example, it cannot be encoded); nothing was appended.                                                     | —             |\n   567\t| `:dao.stream/closed`          | What this handle is on is closed to new appends; the value was not appended.                                                                         | —             |\n   568\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the append; nothing was appended.                                                                                    | —             |\n   569\t\n   570\tAcceptance is handle-relative, as close outcomes are. **Which sequence** a\n   571\thandle's writer surface is on is part of the transport's declared nature: for a\n   572\tring buffer it is the logical stream itself, so `ok` places the value in it; for\n   573\ta transport that carries values toward a stream elsewhere it is the ordered\n   574\toutbound path, and what becomes of the value at the far end is reported there,\n   575\tnot here. No remote transport can say more — under *no operation waits*, an\n   576\tanswer from the far end cannot arrive before `append!` returns.\n   577\t\n   578\tThe writer decides what to do about any non-`ok` outcome; DaoStream does not\n   579\tretry, buffer, or notify on its behalf.\n   580\t\n   581\t## Close\n   582\t\n   583\t`close!` takes a handle and returns a map whose outcome set is `{ok}` — no\n   584\tother outcome exists for it. It is idempotent: closing an already-closed scope\n   585\tis also `:dao.stream/ok`.\n   805\tOD-1 to OD-4 are ordered by cost of delay. Each is a paragraph today, while\n   806\tthree transports and a handful of consumers exist, and a migration once a\n   807\tdurable or replicated transport ships. OD-5 was added afterwards and is\n   808\tnumbered by arrival; it concerns the layers above this contract rather than the\n   809\tcontract's own operations.\n   810\t\n   811\t### OD-1. An unrecognized outcome has no defined meaning\n   812\t\n   813\t**The assumption.** The Result Convention makes each operation's outcome set\n   814\texhaustive and makes result *maps* open. The two rules are asymmetric: a future\n   815\textension may add a key, never an outcome. The contract assumes the seven\n   816\toutcome sets are complete for every transport that will ever exist.\n   817\t\n   818\t**Evidence.** Consumers are already total over the closed sets and treat\n   819\tanything else as a fault: `dao.stream.observe/step` classifies an unrecognized\n   820\tread or effect outcome as `transport-error` and reports `:defect` or `:failed`;\n   821\t`yin.vm.engine/handle-put` and `handle-next` throw. *Envelopes* declines to\n   822\treserve an outcome for gating and says gating \"arrives as an addition\", but\n   823\tunder the exhaustive rule a new outcome is not an addition.\n   824\t\n   825\t**What breaks.** Any condition the present outcomes cannot express honestly:\n   826\tattachment or append refused for lack of authority; a descriptor that is valid\n   827\tbut served elsewhere now; a transport that is throttling rather than full; an\n   828\tappend whose effect is unknown (OD-2). Each must either be squeezed into an\n   829\texisting outcome that misdescribes it (`not-found` for unauthorized, `full` for\n   830\tthrottled) or break every consumer.\n   831\t\n   832\t**Proposed resolution.** Keep each outcome set exhaustive *for this version of\n   833\tthe contract* and add one rule to the Result Convention that makes later\n   834\toutcomes safe to introduce:\n   835\t\n   836\t> A consumer that receives an outcome it does not recognize treats it as: not\n   837\t> `ok`; nothing observed and nothing appended; and not retryable, unless the\n   838\t> result map carries `:dao.stream/retry? true`. It does not throw, because an\n   839\t> unrecognized outcome is a newer contract, not a defect in this host's\n   840\t> assembly.\n   841\t\n   842\tWith that rule a newer transport degrades to \"refused, do not retry\" on an\n   843\tolder consumer instead of to an exception, and `:dao.stream/retry?` lets it\n   844\tsay when waiting is the right response. The existing `full` and `blocked` keep\n   845\ttheir meanings and need not carry the key.\n   846\t\n   847\t**Cost if deferred.** Every consumer written in the meantime hard-codes\n   848\t\"unknown means defect\", and the first new outcome is a coordinated change\n   849\tacross all of them.\n   850\t\n   851\t### OD-2. `append!` assumes its own effect is always knowable\n   852\t\n   853\t**The assumption.** *Writing* defines `transport-error` as \"the transport\n   854\tfailed to perform the append; nothing was appended\", and `ok` as acceptance at\n   855\ta definite position. Both assume the transport knows, at return time, whether\n   856\tthe value is in the sequence.\n   857\t\n   858\t**Evidence.** True of all three transports today: `ringbuffer` and `memory-log`\n   859\tare single in-memory state transitions, and `ws` sidesteps the question because\n   860\tits writer surface is the outbound path, so `ok` claims only enqueueing. No\n   861\ttransport yet has a medium that can fail *after* accepting bytes.\n   862\t\n   863\t**What breaks.** A durable file whose write succeeds and whose sync fails; a\n   864\tlog replicated to a quorum; an append forwarded to a remote sequencer. Each has\n   865\ta third state, *unknown*, that the table cannot express. A consumer written\n   866\tagainst the present text retries on `transport-error`, which is correct only if\n   867\tnothing was appended, and duplicates the value when something was.\n   868\t\n   869\tTwo related gaps compound it. `append!`'s `ok` carries no correlation value, so\n   870\twhere the real answer is displaced to another channel (*Writing*: \"what becomes\n   871\tof the value at the far end is reported there, not here\") nothing in the\n   872\tcontract ties that answer to the append that caused it. `attach!` has\n   873\t`:dao.stream/attachment` for exactly this; `append!` has no counterpart. And\n   874\t`ok` promises neither readability nor delivery nor durability, with no named\n   875\tway to learn any of the three later.\n   876\t\n   877\t**Proposed resolution.** Three sentences, no new operation:\n   878\t\n   879\t1. Reword `transport-error` on `append!`: \"The transport failed to perform the\n   880\t   append. Whether the value was appended is unknown unless the transport\n   881\t   declares that its failures are clean.\" The three existing transports declare\n   882\t   clean failure, so nothing changes for them.\n   883\t2. State the rule the RPC layer already follows: **deduplication and\n   884\t   correlation are the payload's.** A writer that must retry safely puts an\n   885\t   identity in the value; a reader that must not act twice deduplicates on it.\n   886\t   The stream never promises exactly-once.\n   887\t3. Name where later answers land: a transport whose acceptance is not its\n   888\t   final answer (durability reached, far-end refusal) declares the channel on\n   889\t   which that answer is deposited, as *Close* already requires for a failing\n   890\t   flush.\n   891\t\n   892\t**Cost if deferred.** The first durable transport either lies (reports clean\n   893\tfailure it cannot guarantee) or changes the meaning of an outcome that every\n   894\tretry loop already depends on.\n   895\t\n   896\t### OD-3. A cursor binds to the logical stream but is shaped by one transport\n   897\t\n   898\t**The assumption.** *Cursors* promises that \"any handle on the same logical\n   899\tstream accepts it\", and *Envelopes* allows that \"different descriptors may\n   900\treach the same logical stream through different endpoints\". *Cursors* also says\n   901\t\"a cursor's representation belongs to the transport\". Together these hold only\n   902\tif a logical stream lives on exactly one transport for its whole life.\n   903\t\n   904\t**Evidence.** Cursor maps are keyed in the minting transport's namespace\n   905\t(`:dao.stream.ringbuffer/identity` and `/position`,\n   906\t`:dao.stream.memory-log/identity` and `/position`), so a cursor from one\n   907\ttransport is `invalid-cursor` on any other, whatever identity it names. Across\n   908\thosts the promise has never been exercised: `ws` has no reader surface, so\n   909\tinbound values are deposited on a local stream with its own identity and its\n   910\town positions. What a remote host reads is a copy, not the stream. Finally,\n   911\t\"whether a cursor survives serialization to another host is transport-owned and\n   912\tTBD\", while *Explicitly Absent* removes `seek`: a kept cursor is therefore the\n   913\tonly checkpoint a consumer has, and whether it can be written down is\n   914\tundecided.\n   915\t\n   916\t**What breaks.** Replication and DHT replicas (which replica's positions are\n   917\tthe stream's?); re-homing a stream from memory to a durable medium without\n   918\tinvalidating every kept cursor; a durable consumer resuming after restart;\n   919\tcomparing two replicas to detect a forked history.\n   920\t\n   921\t**Proposed resolution.** Two decisions, the first a choice:\n   922\t\n   923\t1. **Choose what a logical identity ranges over.** Either\n   924\t   (a) *one origin, one transport*: a logical stream is the sequence one\n   925\t   transport instance owns. A copy on another host or medium is a different\n   926\t   logical stream with its own identity; that it mirrors the first is a fact for an\n   927\t   interpreter to record. This is how the code already behaves:\n   928\t   `dao.stream.forward` copies into a destination with its own identity. The\n   929\t   sentence\n   930\t   about different descriptors reaching one stream narrows to \"different\n   931\t   endpoints of the same owning transport\". Or\n   932\t   (b) *contract-level position*: the contract defines a position within an\n   933\t   identity (an ordinal), cursors carry it under a `:dao.stream/…` key, and any\n   934\t   transport serving that identity honors it.\n   935\t   Recommended: (a). It matches Axiom 2 (sameness between sequences is an\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '1,335p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-25 11:39:17 GMT\n     2\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 18:39:17 +07\n     3\t\n     4\t# dao.stream.serve — Serving Any Stream Over a Network\n     5\t\n     6\tStatus: proposed design target, subordinate to `dao.stream.md`. That document is the contract; this one specifies how a handle on any transport is made attachable from another host, over WebSocket or UDP, including behind NAT and peer to peer. Where the two disagree, the contract wins. Draft text is ready to become `docs/design/dao.stream.serve.md`.\n     7\t\n     8\t## 1. The problem stated in contract terms\n     9\t\n    10\tThe contract separates a **handle** (host-local, the protocol implementation), a **logical-stream identity** (plain data, the same through every handle), a **descriptor** (reachability data naming the stream) and a **cursor** (immutable, interpreter-owned, bound to the logical stream). A handle never travels. A descriptor does. A cursor does once OD-3.2 is accepted.\n    11\t\n    12\tToday one transport crosses a network, and it does not serve a stream; it serves a **copy** of one. `dao.stream.serving` forwards the source's values into a socket; the far side's adapter deposits them into a local medium with the local medium's identity and positions (`dao.stream.md`, OD-3 evidence: \"what a remote host reads is a copy, not the stream\"). That is the right shape for broadcast and it is the wrong shape for the Universal Continuation Format, whose cells travel with a **kept cursor** into the **original** logical stream and must observe the source's own `gap` when the source evicts (§7.4.3, §7.5.3).\n    13\t\n    14\tSo what must cross the network is not values but **operations and outcomes**. The contract already makes this possible: every operation's result is an open plain-data map, every outcome set is exhaustive, `next` is non-destructive and keyed by a cursor, and the two existing cursor shapes are plain data. The contract is a wire protocol that has not yet been sent. This document sends it.\n    15\t\n    16\tOwner requirements, restated as design obligations:\n    17\t\n    18\t1. A string can have a `dao.stream` interface, and that stream must become network-accessible without its implementation knowing. Obligation: **mechanical derivation** — the server side is computed from the handle and its declared surface, with no per-transport code.\n    19\t2. Over WebSocket or UDP. Obligation: the protocol is defined over an abstract **channel**, with WebSocket and UDP as two channels and no protocol change between them.\n    20\t3. Behind NAT. Obligation: serving is decoupled from listening; a host that can only dial out can still serve.\n    21\t4. P2P over both, behind NAT. Obligation: the protocol is **symmetric** with respect to who dialed, and the reachability model includes rendezvous, hole punching, and relay fallback.\n    22\t\n    23\t## 2. Layering\n    24\t\n    25\tFour layers, dependencies one-way, downward only:\n    26\t\n    27\t```\n    28\tcontract-generic reader/writer code           sees a handle; knows nothing below\n    29\t  │\n    30\tproxy handle + proxy-step   ─┐\n    31\t  │  serve frames (data)     │  dao.stream.serve — this document\n    32\tserve step + serve table    ─┘\n    33\t  │\n    34\tchannel                       a duplex message path with a deposit-model adapter\n    35\t                              (ws today; udp new; a channel over a channel for relay)\n    36\t  │\n    37\treachability                  how a channel is established: direct, relayed, punched\n    38\t                              (descriptor candidates; rendezvous is a channel peer)\n    39\t```\n    40\t\n    41\tRules that hold across the layers:\n    42\t\n    43\t- **The serve layer owns the vocabulary of operations, sessions and outcomes.** A channel carries opaque application values and lifecycle events. No channel inspects a serve frame; no serve frame names a channel.\n    44\t- **Reachability lives in the descriptor** (contract, Envelopes) as an ordered list of channel candidates. The serve layer never interprets an address; it hands each candidate to the host's channel dispatch.\n    45\t- **Every layer is stepped.** Proxy, server, relay and rendezvous are pure transitions over explicit state driven by a composition-owned driver that passes `now`. Nothing waits, nothing schedules itself, nothing registers a callback above the channel adapter. This is OD-5's rule applied to a whole subsystem.\n    46\t\n    47\t## 3. The serve protocol\n    48\t\n    49\t### 3.1 Sessions\n    50\t\n    51\tA **session** is one attachment to one served logical stream over one channel. It is the serve-layer unit that carries `:dao.stream/attachment`. One channel carries many sessions, tagged by `:serve/session`, minted by the opening side and unique per channel. Multiplexing is here rather than in the channel so that channels stay dumb and so that one channel between two peers carries every stream a migrated continuation references.\n    52\t\n    53\tRoles are per session, not per channel: on one channel, peer A may serve stream X to B while B serves stream Y to A. This is the P2P case and needs no extra mechanism.\n    54\t\n    55\t### 3.2 Frames\n    56\t\n    57\tServe frames are the application values a channel carries. Keys are qualified under `:serve/…` with one separator, per the ws rule. The operation and its arguments ride in the `dao.stream.apply` envelope, which already owns request identity, operation and arguments:\n    58\t\n    59\t```clojure\n    60\t;; session control\n    61\t{:serve/frame :serve/open     :serve/session s  :dao.stream/identity id}\n    62\t{:serve/frame :serve/opened   :serve/session s  :dao.stream/attachment a\n    63\t :dao.stream/surface #{:reader :writer :closable}\n    64\t :serve/oldest <cursor>  :serve/newest <cursor>}                       ; anchors as of open, §3.6\n    65\t{:serve/frame :serve/disclaim :serve/session s}                        ; the authoritative not-found\n    66\t{:serve/frame :serve/close    :serve/session s}                        ; either direction, terminal\n    67\t\n    68\t;; operations\n    69\t{:serve/frame :serve/request  :serve/session s\n    70\t :dao.stream.apply/id n  :dao.stream.apply/op :dao.stream/next  :dao.stream.apply/args [cursor budget]}\n    71\t{:serve/frame :serve/response :serve/session s\n    72\t :dao.stream.apply/id n  :dao.stream.apply/ok <see 3.3>\n    73\t :serve/oldest <cursor>  :serve/newest <cursor>}                       ; piggybacked refresh, §3.6\n    74\t\n    75\t;; channel liveness (per channel, not per session)\n    76\t{:serve/frame :serve/ping :serve/nonce k}\n    77\t{:serve/frame :serve/pong :serve/nonce k}\n    78\t\n    79\t;; reachability control, §7\n    80\t{:serve/frame :serve/register  :serve/peer p}                          ; outbound-serving host → relay or rendezvous\n    81\t{:serve/frame :serve/dial      :serve/peer p  :serve/link l}           ; client → relay/rendezvous: reach p\n    82\t{:serve/frame :serve/introduce :serve/peer q  :serve/link l  :serve/reflexive {…}}   ; rendezvous → p\n    83\t{:serve/frame :serve/via       :serve/link l  :serve/inner <frame>}    ; relayed carriage, §6.4\n    84\t{:serve/frame :serve/punch     :serve/link l  :serve/nonce k}          ; hole-punch probe, §7.4\n    85\t```\n    86\t\n    87\tA frame with an unknown `:serve/frame`, a missing required key, or a value outside the channel's portable domain is a protocol failure of the **channel** (decode diagnostic then teardown), never a serve outcome: the serve layer only ever sees valid frames, exactly as the ws adapter only deposits valid events.\n    88\t\n    89\t### 3.3 Mechanical derivation\n    90\t\n    91\tThe server for a handle `h` with declared surface `S` is the apply handler table below, restricted to `S`. Nothing about `h`'s transport appears anywhere; a string-backed stream, a ring buffer and a durable log are served by the same six lines.\n    92\t\n    93\t| Wire op                | Args                     | Server computes                          | `:dao.stream.apply/ok` value                              |\n    94\t|------------------------|--------------------------|------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|\n    95\t| `:dao.stream/descriptor` | `[]`                   | `(descriptor h)`                         | the outcome map, verbatim                                 |\n    96\t| `:dao.stream/cursor`   | `[anchor]`               | `(cursor h anchor)`                      | the outcome map, verbatim                                 |\n    97\t| `:dao.stream/next`     | `[cursor budget]`        | `next` repeated from `cursor`, following each `ok`'s successor, at most `budget` times, stopping at the first non-`ok` | a vector of outcome maps, in order, verbatim |\n    98\t| `:dao.stream/append!`  | `[value]`                | `(append! h value)`                      | the outcome map, verbatim                                 |\n    99\t| `:dao.stream/close!`   | `[]`                     | `(close! h)` only if `h` is an attachment the server minted for this session; never the owner's close | `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}` |\n   100\t\n   101\tAn op absent from `S` is answered with an apply **error** response, code `:dao.stream.serve/no-surface`: the contract forbids answering a nonexistent operation with an outcome map (Surfaces), and the apply envelope has an error shape for exactly this. The proxy never exposes such an op, so the error only ever reaches a misbehaving client.\n   102\t\n   103\tOutcome maps cross **verbatim**, including the cursors inside them. The serve layer never constructs, parses or rewrites a cursor. This is the whole basis of cursor-namespace preservation: the proxy hands the reader exactly the cursor the source minted, and hands the source exactly the cursor the reader held.\n   104\t\n   105\t**Precondition on the source.** Its cursors and outcome maps must lie in the channel's portable value domain. Both existing transports qualify (a string identity and an integer position). A transport whose cursor holds a host object cannot be served; the exporter refuses it before publishing (§9). This is the whole content of the UCF's \"portable cursor profile\": one profile, `:dao.stream.serve/v1`, meaning *cursor is plain data in the portable domain and the source honors it after serialization*.\n   106\t\n   107\t### 3.4 Idempotence, loss and duplication\n   108\t\n   109\tFour of the five operations are idempotent as the contract defines them: `descriptor` and `cursor` are projections; `next` is non-destructive and positioned by its argument; `close!` is idempotent by definition. A lost request is re-sent; a lost response is re-requested; a duplicated request re-computes the same answer or a later, equally true one. **No sequence numbers, acknowledgement vectors or sliding windows are needed for reads: the cursor is the sequence number, and `gap` remains the source's declaration rather than a transport artifact.** This is what reconciles UDP with kept cursors and gap, and why v1 DRDS (`daostream-udp-design.md`) is not revived.\n   110\t\n   111\t`append!` is the one non-idempotent operation. The protocol makes it safe by the rule OD-2 already states — deduplication and correlation are the payload's — applied at the serve layer so that no application has to:\n   112\t\n   113\t- A session has **at most one outstanding `append!`**. The proxy does not issue the next until the previous is answered or the session is lost. This is `dao.stream.rpc`'s `unsent` discipline, and it also makes UDP reordering harmless for writes.\n   114\t- The server keeps, per session, the id and outcome of the **last** `append!` it performed. A request with that id is answered from the record without re-appending. Window size one follows from the one-outstanding rule.\n   115\t- If the session is lost with an append unanswered, the proxy reports it as `:dao.stream.serve/append-unknown` on its event medium with the retained value. The proxy does not silently retry across sessions: the far end may have appended. This is OD-2's third state named honestly.\n   116\t\n   117\tA stale response (an id no longer outstanding) is a diagnostic, not an error, exactly as in `dao.stream.rpc`.\n   118\t\n   119\t### 3.5 Batching\n   120\t\n   121\t`next`'s `budget` bounds one response. The server answers with consecutive outcomes; the proxy installs each `(cursor_i → outcome_i)` with `cursor_0` the requested cursor and `cursor_{i+1}` the successor in `outcome_i`. A server also bounds the batch by the channel's frame budget (§6.3): it stops early when the next value would not fit and returns what it has. A single value that cannot fit in one frame on this channel is answered as `transport-error` with `:dao.stream.serve/reason :oversize` in place of that element; the read is not silently skipped and the cursor does not advance past it. Large media belong in the payload as `dao.jing` addresses (contract, Granularity).\n   122\t\n   123\t### 3.6 Anchors across a hop\n   124\t\n   125\t`cursor` has no `blocked` outcome, yet a proxy cannot consult the source synchronously. The design does not invent a proxy-namespaced cursor (that would put a cursor in circulation that the source's own handle rejects, violating \"any handle on the same logical stream accepts it\"). Instead the server **piggybacks fresh `:oldest` and `:newest` cursors on `:serve/opened` and on every response**, and the proxy's `cursor` answers with the most recently observed one.\n   126\t\n   127\tThis is honest under the contract's own definitions. A stale `:newest` is *earlier* than a fresh one, so a composition that mints `:newest` before invoking an operation still observes everything the operation causes; it may additionally observe a few values appended between the last exchange and the mint, which is re-seeing, never skipping. A stale `:oldest` may point at a position since evicted, and the contract already says a fresh `:oldest` is a position rather than a completeness claim: the reader receives `gap` with the recovery cursor. Before the first `:serve/opened` the proxy holds no anchors and `cursor` answers `transport-error` with `:dao.stream/retry? true` (OD-1's proposed key); the composition observes `:serve/opened` on the proxy's event medium and mints then.\n   128\t\n   129\t### 3.7 Held reads (optional, v1 decision needed)\n   130\t\n   131\tAcross a network a `blocked` answer costs a round trip. A `next` request may carry `:serve/hold true`; a server that honors it records the demand in session state and, on each of its own driver steps, re-polls the source, answering when the outcome is no longer `blocked` or when its hold budget (a constructor policy in the driver's clock domain) elapses, in which case it answers the `blocked` it has. No operation waits: the session holds state, the step returns. A proxy must tolerate a server that ignores the flag. Whether v1 includes this is an owner decision (Q2).\n   132\t\n   133\t## 4. The proxy handle: declared nature\n   134\t\n   135\tA proxy is a handle minted by `attach!` on a serve descriptor. It declares exactly the surface the `:serve/opened` frame reports, which is the source's declared surface; until then it declares the surface the descriptor's `:serve/surface` hint names, and a mismatch at open is a session failure deposited as data. `descriptor` returns the serve descriptor and the identity. The proxy is composed as three explicit pieces: the handle (an object over an explicit state atom, as `WsHandle` is), an **event medium** the composition wires and the channel adapter deposits into, and `proxy-step`, which the composition's driver calls with `now`.\n   136\t\n   137\tThe handle's `next` is a read of the proxy's **window**, a bounded host-local cache keyed by cursor value (structural equality; every cursor a reader holds was minted by the source, through this proxy or another). A miss records a **demand** in proxy state and answers `blocked`. `proxy-step` issues demands as requests, installs responses into the window, opens sessions, retries by the composition's policy, and evicts the window oldest-first. The window is a cache, not retention: an evicted window entry is re-fetched, and only the source ever answers `gap`.\n   138\t\n   139\t| Op        | Produces                                                                 | Excluded, and why |\n   140\t|-----------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------|\n   141\t| `cursor`  | `ok` (last observed anchor, §3.6), `invalid-anchor` (relayed), `closed`, `transport-error` (`:establishing`, retryable; or channel failure) | — |\n   142\t| `next`    | every source outcome, relayed verbatim; `blocked` also for *not here yet*; `end` for positions beyond the window after the session ended; `cursor-mismatch` locally by identity; `invalid-cursor` for a non-map; `transport-error` for `:oversize` or channel failure | — |\n   143\t| `append!` | `ok` (accepted into the session's outbound path, carrying `:dao.stream.serve/id`), `full` (one append already outstanding, or channel outbound full; transient), `invalid-value` (outside the channel's domain), `closed`, `transport-error` | — |\n   144\t| `close!`  | `ok`                                                                    | — |\n   145\t| `descriptor` | `ok`                                                                 | — |\n   146\t\n   147\tThe proxy's writer surface is on the **session's ordered outbound path**, as ws's is on the connection's; the source's answer to an append arrives on the proxy's event medium as `{:serve/event :serve/appended :dao.stream.serve/id n :serve/outcome <map>}`. This is the contract's Writing rule and the ws precedent, and it has a cost for the UCF (Q1).\n   148\t\n   149\tSession loss is detachment, not ending: the proxy deposits `:serve/detached`, `append!` answers `closed`, and `next` answers what the window holds and then `end`, exactly as the contract specifies for a closed attachment. Cursors bind to the logical stream, so the reader's kept cursor survives; a fresh `attach!` with the same descriptor resumes at it. Re-establishing a lost session under the same proxy handle, answering `blocked` meanwhile, is a **proxy policy the composition supplies as data** (`:reconnect {:attempts n :after-ms t}`); the default is none, so nothing retries invisibly.\n   150\t\n   151\tTwo subtleties the contract forces:\n   152\t\n   153\t- `end` from the source is relayed. `end` from session loss is local. Both mean \"what this handle is on is closed\" and both are correct; a program that reads `end` as *the stream ended* will misread a channel loss unless its composition reconnects. This is the same limit ws documents under *Ending a served stream*, and the same answer: the serve `:serve/close` frame from the server carries `:serve/reason :ended` when the source's owner closed it, so the proxy can deposit `:serve/ended` versus `:serve/detached` and a composition can tell which it saw.\n   154\t- A proxy is not a retaining medium and has no `gap` of its own. It never produces a `gap` the source did not.\n   155\t\n   156\t## 5. The server\n   157\t\n   158\tServer state is a value: `{:served {identity → {:handle h :surface S}} :sessions {session-id → {:identity … :attachment a :last-append {…} :held {…}}}}`. `serve-step` takes the state, one inbound frame or the driver's `now`, and returns the next state plus zero or more frames to send. The composition owns the driver and the channel writer.\n   159\t\n   160\tThe **serve table** is composition data, not a registry: the host decides what it serves, adds and removes entries through `serve`/`unserve` transitions on its own state, and the table's lifetime is the host's business. Nothing here is namespace-global and nothing is consulted ambiently, which is the contract's \"no stream table\" preserved: a descriptor naming an identity this server does not hold is answered `:serve/disclaim`, the authoritative `not-found`.\n   161\t\n   162\tThe server is **stateless with respect to reads**. It holds no reader positions; every `next` carries its cursor. This is what lets it survive a client's silence, a duplicate request, or a session that was lost and reopened, without any resumption protocol: resumption is the client re-sending the cursor it kept.\n   163\t\n   164\tThe server never calls the owner's `close!`. A session's `:dao.stream/close!` closes only an attachment handle the server minted for that session; for a source that mints no attachments (a ring buffer, a memory log) it is a session-scoped no-op, and the session ends. Ending the served stream is its owner's act on the owner's handle, observed by the server as `end` from `next` and relayed.\n   165\t\n   166\t## 6. Channels\n   167\t\n   168\t### 6.1 Channel contract\n   169\t\n   170\tThe serve layer needs exactly what `dao.stream.ws` already provides and nothing more:\n   171\t\n   172\t1. A **writer handle** whose `append!` places one application value on an ordered (WebSocket) or unordered (UDP) outbound path, with `ok`, `full`, `invalid-value`, `closed`, `transport-error`.\n   173\t2. A **deposit-model adapter** that turns every inbound application value and every lifecycle event into one plain-data event on a medium the composition wired, correlated by an attachment identity, judging none of them.\n   174\t3. A **portable value domain** and a **frame budget** (bytes per value; unbounded for WebSocket, the datagram budget for UDP).\n   175\t4. A declaration of **ordering** (`:ordered` or `:unordered`) and **reliability** (`:reliable` or `:best-effort`), which the proxy's retry policy reads as composition data.\n   176\t\n   177\tThe channel's declared nature is what changes the proxy's policy, never its protocol.\n   178\t\n   179\t### 6.2 WebSocket channel\n   180\t\n   181\t`dao.stream.ws` is used as is, with one composition and one wording change:\n   182\t\n   183\t- **Composition.** A serve endpoint serves one path (by convention `/dao.stream.serve`) whose \"served stream\" is the channel itself: the descriptor's `:dao.stream/identity` is the serving **peer id**, not a stream. The accepted attachment's traffic medium feeds `serve-step`; the accepted socket handle is `serve-step`'s writer. `dao.stream.serving` is not used: it forwards a source into every accepted socket, which is the copy model. A small sibling composition, `dao.stream.serve.ws`, wires an accepted attachment to `serve-step` instead of to `forward-step`.\n   184\t- **Direction.** A host that dials **out** with `ws/attach!` and then runs `serve-step` over that attachment is serving. Nothing in the ws state machine cares who dialed; only its prose does (§ Contradictions, C1).\n   185\t\n   186\tHosts: clj (JDK client; server via the existing endpoint seam), cljs browser (client only; no listener exists), cljs node (both), cljd (both under `dart:io`; Flutter web is a browser).\n   187\t\n   188\t### 6.3 UDP channel\n   189\t\n   190\t`dao.stream.udp` is a new channel with the ws deposit model and no reader surface: a socket retains nothing. It is the datagram boundary the DHT node already uses, reshaped into the adapter form.\n   191\t\n   192\t- **Descriptor.** `{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/udp :dao.stream/identity <peer-id> :udp/host … :udp/port …}`. `attach!` binds a local socket (ephemeral port) and returns a handle whose writer path is \"datagrams to that address\". No handshake is needed at this layer; the serve layer's `:serve/open` is the handshake.\n   193\t- **One value per datagram**, encoded with the CBOR codec profile (binary, canonical, already specified in ws). Frame budget 1200 bytes, the DHT's figure, chosen to clear common path MTUs without fragmentation. A value over budget is `invalid-value` on `append!` and, from the server's side, `:oversize` (§3.5). IP fragmentation is not relied on; application fragmentation is deferred and, if added, lives in this channel, invisible to the serve layer.\n   194\t- **Declared nature:** `:unordered`, `:best-effort`. The proxy's retry policy therefore has real work: each outstanding request records `:sent-at`; `proxy-step` re-sends when `now - sent-at` exceeds `:retry-after-ms`, with `:max-attempts`, both composition data. Exhaustion is session loss, deposited as data.\n   195\t- **Lifecycle events** are thinner than ws: there is no peer close, so `:udp/closed` is deposited only on local close or deposit failure; peer silence is the serve layer's business (`:serve/ping` and the retry policy), not the channel's.\n   196\t- **Attachment identity** is the remote `(host, port)` pair as seen by the socket; replies go to the datagram's source address, never to a claimed address in the payload, as the DHT already rules.\n   197\t- Hosts: clj (`DatagramSocket`), cljs node (`dgram`), cljd (`RawDatagramSocket`). **Browsers have no UDP.** The browser's P2P path is a WebSocket to a relay (§7.3) today and a WebRTC DataChannel channel later (§14); both slot into §6.1 without touching the serve layer.\n   198\t\n   199\t### 6.4 A channel over a channel\n   200\t\n   201\tA relayed path is a channel whose writer wraps every value as `{:serve/frame :serve/via :serve/link l :serve/inner v}` and whose adapter unwraps `:serve/via` frames with its link from the underlying channel's medium, depositing `:serve/inner`. It declares the underlying channel's ordering and reliability. Nothing else is new: streams are values that travel through streams, and a channel is a stream. The relay itself (§7.3) sees only `:serve/via` and a link table.\n   202\t\n   203\t## 7. Reachability\n   204\t\n   205\t### 7.1 The serve descriptor\n   206\t\n   207\t```clojure\n   208\t{:dao.stream/type      :dao.stream/serve\n   209\t :dao.stream/identity  \"…\"                                   ; the served logical stream\n   210\t :serve/peer           \"…\"                                   ; the serving peer, opaque string\n   211\t :serve/surface        #{:reader}                            ; hint; :serve/opened is authoritative\n   212\t :serve/candidates     [{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/ws  :ws/host \"a.example\" :ws/port 9090 :ws/path \"/dao.stream.serve\" :dao.stream/identity \"peer-A\"}\n   213\t                        {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/udp :udp/host \"203.0.113.5\" :udp/port 4000 :dao.stream/identity \"peer-A\"}\n   214\t                        {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/udp :udp/host \"r.example\" :udp/port 4000 :dao.stream/identity \"relay-R\" :serve/via :punch :serve/peer \"peer-A\"}\n   215\t                        {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/ws  :ws/host \"r.example\" :ws/port 9090 :ws/path \"/dao.stream.serve\" :dao.stream/identity \"relay-R\" :serve/via :relay :serve/peer \"peer-A\"}]}\n   216\t```\n   217\t\n   218\tEach candidate is an ordinary channel descriptor, optionally marked `:serve/via :relay` or `:punch` with the target peer. The proxy tries candidates in order; each attempt's outcome is data on the proxy's event medium (`:serve/candidate-failed` with the index and the channel's own resolution event), and the first channel that yields `:serve/opened` is the session's. Sequential trial is a v1 simplification; parallel trial with a preference order (ICE's shape) is an additive proxy policy. The descriptor carries no authorization, as the contract requires.\n   219\t\n   220\t### 7.2 Direct\n   221\t\n   222\tThe serving host listens; the candidate is a plain ws or udp descriptor. This is the existing ws model and the only one `dao.stream.ws.md` describes.\n   223\t\n   224\t### 7.3 Relay: outbound-only serving\n   225\t\n   226\tA host behind NAT dials a **relay** R (any host with a reachable address running `relay-step`) and sends `:serve/register` with its peer id, then keeps the channel alive with `:serve/ping`. A client dials R and sends `:serve/dial` naming the peer and a link id it minted. R's state is `{:peers {peer-id → channel-writer} :links {link → [writer-a writer-b]}}`; on `:serve/dial` it pairs the link with the registered peer's channel and thereafter forwards `:serve/via` frames between the two writers by link, copying and judging nothing. Both sides then run the serve protocol over the §6.4 channel. R never sees a session or an operation. A relay is a forwarder: its step is `forward-step` with a link-keyed destination.\n   227\t\n   228\tThis is the browser's whole NAT story and every host's fallback. Its costs are honest: the relay carries every byte, and a registered peer is reachable only while its outbound channel lives, so `:serve/ping` cadence is bounded by NAT binding lifetime (tens of seconds for UDP, longer for TCP). A relay is composition, not infrastructure: anyone runs one, and `dao.stream.discovery`'s rendezvous topics are how a peer finds one.\n   229\t\n   230\t### 7.4 Rendezvous and UDP hole punching\n   231\t\n   232\tBoth peers behind NAT, both with UDP. Each registers with a rendezvous S over UDP; S records the **reflexive address** (source host and port as S observed them) per peer. On `:serve/dial`, S sends `:serve/introduce` to both peers carrying the other's reflexive address and the link. Each peer then sends `:serve/punch` probes with the link's nonce to the other's reflexive address on a schedule `proxy-step` reads from `now`; the first probe to arrive opens the NAT binding in the other direction, and the first `:serve/punch` received is answered with a `:serve/pong`. When both sides have a pong the direct udp channel exists and the serve session opens over it. If no pong arrives within the punch budget, the proxy falls to the next candidate, normally the relay through the same S (a rendezvous is also a relay, and one channel to it serves both roles).\n   233\t\n   234\tThis is STUN and ICE reduced to what the datom.world model needs: reflexive addresses are facts deposited by S, probes are values, and the state machine is a step. Symmetric NATs that assign a fresh port per destination defeat punching and take the relay path; that is a property of the network, reported as a failed candidate, not a failure of the protocol.\n   235\t\n   236\t### 7.5 P2P symmetry\n   237\t\n   238\tOnce any channel exists between A and B, each may `:serve/open` sessions on the other's serve table, in both directions, on the same channel. There is no client host and server host, only per-session roles. A continuation migrating from A to B whose cells reference streams on A is served by A over the very channel B dialed to fetch it. This falls out of §3.1 and needs no additional design.\n   239\t\n   240\t### 7.6 Liveness\n   241\t\n   242\t`:serve/ping` and `:serve/pong` are per channel. Cadence and the number of unanswered pings that end a channel are composition data in the driver's clock domain. A channel ended by silence deposits its lifecycle event; every session on it is lost (§4). This resolves the ws document's deferred *Liveness* item for channels used by this layer, with ordinary values rather than protocol frames, because a protocol frame would be a fact deposited by nobody.\n   243\t\n   244\t## 8. Host isolation\n   245\t\n   246\t| Piece                                  | clj | cljs browser | cljs node | cljd | Where the host code lives |\n   247\t|----------------------------------------|-----|--------------|-----------|------|---------------------------|\n   248\t| serve frames, proxy, server, relay, rendezvous steps | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | `.cljc`, pure data, no host code |\n   249\t| ws channel, dial out                   | ✓   | ✓            | ✓         | ✓    | existing `:connect!` seams |\n   250\t| ws channel, listen                     | ✓   | —            | ✓         | ✓    | existing endpoint seam |\n   251\t| udp channel                            | ✓   | —            | ✓         | ✓    | new socket seam, same shape as ws's `:send!`/`:close!` |\n   252\t| relayed channel (§6.4)                 | ✓   | ✓            | ✓         | ✓    | `.cljc` |\n   253\t| hole punching                          | ✓   | —            | ✓         | ✓    | `.cljc` over the udp seam |\n   254\t| WebRTC DataChannel (deferred)          | —   | later        | —         | later (Flutter) | a future channel |\n   255\t\n   256\tA browser peer therefore serves and proxies through a relay; a browser can be a P2P participant behind NAT, just not a hole-punching one until a DataChannel channel exists.\n   257\t\n   258\t## 9. UCF integration\n   259\t\n   260\tWhat §7.4.3 and §7.5.3 should say, replacing the undefined \"standard stream-over-network facade\":\n   261\t\n   262\t- **Lift.** For each stream cell, the exporter checks: the handle's declared surface covers what the cell needs; its cursors and outcome maps are in the portable domain (`:dao.stream.serve/v1`, the one cursor profile); and the exporter has at least one reachability candidate. It adds `identity → {handle, surface}` to its serve table and writes a serve descriptor into the cell's `:yin.k/stream`. The cell's `:yin.k/position` is the kept cursor, verbatim. Any check failing is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` before minting, as the UCF already says.\n   263\t- **Lower.** The resumer `attach!`s each serve descriptor, receiving a proxy. `next` at the kept cursor is the first demand; `blocked` until served; the source's `gap` arrives verbatim. `:serve/disclaim` on open is `not-found` and hence `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the identity. A candidate list that is exhausted is the same.\n   264\t- **Lifetime.** The exporter must keep the entry served while the migrated task may need it. The correct home for that obligation is a lease (`dao.lease.md`): the served entry is a grant, the resumer renews, and lapse unserves. Until that lease exists, the exporter keeps the entry until its own composition retires it, and a resumer that arrives after retirement gets `not-found`. This is stated as an open question (Q6) rather than designed here.\n   265\t- **Pinning.** Serving from the emitter pins the emitter: the stream is where it is, and OD-3(a) makes a copy a different stream. Re-homing a stream is out of scope and should be said so in the UCF.\n   266\t\n   267\t## 10. Invariant compliance\n   268\t\n   269\t- **No hidden global state.** Serve tables, sessions, links, peers and windows are values inside compositions the host constructs and drives; nothing is namespace-global and no operation consults ambient state.\n   270\t- **No implicit control flow.** Every step is called by a driver with `now`; the only callback code is the channel adapter, which deposits and returns, as the ws adapter does today.\n   271\t- **No raw callbacks.** Resolution, detachment, append outcomes, candidate failures and reflexive addresses are all deposited events, read by cursors.\n   272\t- **No shared mutable state.** A proxy's window and a server's session record are owned by exactly one driver; the source handle is touched only by `serve-step`.\n   273\t- **No layer collapsing.** Relay and rendezvous interpret nothing; the channel interprets nothing; the serve layer interprets operations but never values; the reader interprets values.\n   274\t- **No assumed graphs.** Who serves what to whom is the serve table and the descriptor's candidates, explicit data, never a topology the protocol infers.\n   275\t\n   276\t## 11. Concurrency and linearization\n   277\t\n   278\tThe source is operated only from `serve-step`, in the order requests are dequeued. Reads carry their own cursors, so concurrent readers through one or many proxies neither share nor contend for position. Writes are linearized per session by the one-outstanding rule, and across sessions by the source's own sequence; the contract promises nothing more for concurrent writers and neither does this layer. Over an unordered channel the proxy never issues an `append!` before every earlier request on that session has been answered, so a `:newest` mint that must precede an append does precede it at the source. The relay preserves per-link order only if its underlying channel does; a relayed UDP link is unordered and the proxy's policy already accounts for that through the channel's declaration.\n   279\t\n   280\t## 12. What is contract, transport-owned and deferred\n   281\t\n   282\t- **The contract requires** (and this design depends on): OD-3 resolutions (a) and (2), cursors as plain data that survive serialization; OD-2's payload-correlation rule; OD-1's unrecognized-outcome rule with `:dao.stream/retry?`. None of the three is accepted yet (see C3).\n   283\t- **Transport-owned by this layer:** the frame vocabulary, session identity, the one-outstanding-append rule, anchor piggybacking, held reads, candidate order, retry and reconnect policies, relay and rendezvous state.\n   284\t- **Deliberately deferred:** UDP application fragmentation; parallel candidate trial; WebRTC DataChannel; authorization and postage for `:serve/register` and `:serve/dial` (a relay is a spam surface, and the contract's no-gating stance plus `dao.stream.discovery`'s postage note apply); self-certifying peer ids (v1 peer ids are opaque strings the composition assigns; the discovery document's kickoff-hash identities slot in as an additive rule); a lease over served entries; encryption of the channel (WSS is the host's; UDP has none until a Noise-shaped channel exists, and until then UDP serving is for trusted networks, as the DHT already says).\n   285\t\n   286\t## 13. Completion criteria\n   287\t\n   288\t1. **Loopback conformance.** Serve a memory log and a ring buffer through an in-process channel pair (two ring buffers as the two directions). The proxy passes the contract's reader and writer tests, and every cursor the proxy hands out is structurally equal to one the source minted.\n   289\t2. **Gap fidelity.** Evict at the source under a kept cursor; the proxy relays `gap` with the source's recovery cursor, with no replay and no skip.\n   290\t3. **Anchor semantics.** A `:newest` minted through the proxy never skips a value appended after the mint.\n   291\t4. **UDP under loss, reorder and duplication** (a lossy socket seam): no duplicate append at the source, no lost read, no proxy-fabricated gap, and `:serve/append-unknown` deposited when the session dies with an append in flight.\n   292\t5. **Outbound serving.** A peer that only dials out serves a stream to a peer that dialed the same relay; the relay's state never holds a session.\n   293\t6. **Punch and fallback.** Two UDP peers behind a NAT simulator open a direct channel through a rendezvous; with a symmetric-NAT simulator they fall to the relay candidate, reported as data.\n   294\t7. **P2P symmetry.** Two peers each serve one stream to the other over one channel.\n   295\t8. **UCF §7.11 facade acceptance**, as written there: park with a string-backed stream at a kept cursor, migrate, resume, force eviction, observe `gap`; a non-portable source refuses at lift; an unreachable endpoint refuses at lower.\n   296\t9. **Portability.** Items 1–4 and 7 run on clj, cljs node and cljd; item 5 also on cljs browser through the ws relay path.\n   297\t\n   298\t## 14. Deferred\n   299\t\n   300\tListed in §12. Additionally: a copy-serving mode remains useful (broadcast to many readers with local retention) and is what `dao.stream.serving` already is; it should be renamed or re-described so that \"serving\" no longer suggests attachment to the original stream (C2).\n   301\t\n   302\t---\n   303\t\n   304\t# Open questions for the owner\n   305\t\n   306\t- **Q1. Append through a proxy.** As designed, `append!` on a proxy answers `ok` for acceptance into the session's outbound path and the source's outcome arrives later on the event medium, as ws does and the contract's Writing section allows. The UCF's `:put` rule (\"retries its retained value and resumes on `ok`\") would then resume a program on an append the source may still refuse. Alternative: the proxy answers `full` until the source's outcome arrives and then reports that outcome, holding the value meanwhile, which gives the writer the source's truth from `append!` itself at the cost of a proxy-level convention (one held value, writer retries the same value). Recommendation: keep the contract-consistent design and amend UCF §7.4.3 so that a `:put` on a served stream resumes when the source's outcome is observed, not on outbound `ok`.\n   307\t- **Q2. Held reads in v1.** They cut network chatter for `blocked`-heavy readers such as a parked VM polling a response cell, at the cost of per-session server state and a hold budget. Recommendation: include, since the UCF's dominant pattern is exactly a kept response cursor polled until a value lands.\n   308\t- **Q3. Peer identity.** Opaque composition-assigned strings now, self-certifying kickoff-hash identities later, or self-certifying from the start. Recommendation: opaque now; the relay routes by string equality either way, and signature verification is an additive rule on `:serve/register`.\n   309\t- **Q4. Relay and rendezvous gating.** v1 accepts any `:serve/register` and `:serve/dial`. Recommendation: accept for the dev-only repo, record postage as the intended answer.\n   310\t- **Q5. Accept OD-1, OD-2 and OD-3 (a)+(2) now.** This design is not implementable without OD-3 and is dishonest without OD-2; OD-1 is needed only for the `:establishing` answer. Recommendation: accept all three as a precondition of the implementation phase.\n   311\t- **Q6. Lifetime of a served entry after migration.** Lease-governed (recommended, via `dao.lease`), or composition-retired with `not-found` as the honest late answer.\n   312\t- **Q7. Naming.** `dao.stream.serve` for this layer and a rename of today's `dao.stream.serving` to `dao.stream.serving.copy` or similar, so the two models are not confused.\n   313\t\n   314\t# Contradictions found\n   315\t\n   316\t| Severity | File:line | Evidence | Recommended correction |\n   317\t|---|---|---|---|\n   318\t| high | `docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:18-26` | \"serving is what makes any stream remotely attachable … remote interpreters can attach\" | Only true of copies: the client reads a local medium with local positions (OD-3 evidence, `dao.stream.md:906-911`). Reword to: the ws transport serves copies; attaching to the original stream is `dao.stream.serve`. |\n   319\t| high | `docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:9-16, 455-459` | descriptor \"names a server-hosted stream\"; endpoint constructor is bind-host/advertised-host only; no outbound-serving mode | Add: direction of establishment is independent of the serving role; a dialed-out attachment may carry `serve-step`. The state machine already permits it; only the prose forbids it. |\n   320\t| high | `docs/design/dao.stream.md:523-525` (OD-3), `:851-894` (OD-2), `:811-849` (OD-1) | \"Whether a cursor survives serialization to another host is transport-owned and TBD\"; append's third state undefined; unrecognized outcome undefined | This design requires OD-3 (a)+(2) and OD-2, and uses OD-1's `:dao.stream/retry?`. Accept them; move the text into the sections they amend. |\n   321\t| high | `docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:520-534` | \"standard stream-over-network facade derived from the dao.stream operation and outcome contract … `dao.stream.rpc.ws` demonstrates … does not itself supply the UCF facade\" | Cite `dao.stream.serve.md`; the facade is the serve protocol, the proxy is its handle, and the exporter's obligation is §9 of this design. |\n   322\t| medium | `docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:554-556` | \"a `:put` wait retries its retained value and resumes with it on `ok`\" | Through a proxy, `ok` is outbound acceptance. Resume on the observed source outcome (Q1), or adopt the alternative and say so. |\n   323\t| medium | `docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:780, 929` | `:yin.k/cursor-profiles #{:dao.stream/file-v1}` — a per-transport profile set | One profile, `:dao.stream.serve/v1` (cursor and outcomes in the portable domain). Per-transport profiles would make the resumer know transports, which the proxy exists to prevent. |\n   324\t| medium | `docs/design/daostream-udp-design.md:199-284, 470` | DRDS sequence numbers, ACK vectors, sliding window; \"NAT traversal (STUN / ICE)\" as deferred | Superseded: serve reads are idempotent by cursor, so no DRDS; a datagram stream with its own positions is a different logical stream under OD-3(a). Mark the document superseded prior art; NAT moves to this spec. |\n   325\t| medium | `docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:692-697` | Liveness and resumption protocol listed as deferred | Resumption is answered by kept cursors plus a stateless server (§5); liveness by `:serve/ping` at the serve layer (§7.6). Point the deferred items here. |\n   326\t| low | `docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:165-167` | \"NAT traversal is unaddressed … hole-punching, relay/TURN, and bootstrap discovery\" | Cite §7.3–7.4; a DHT node is a natural rendezvous host and rendezvous topics (`dao.stream.discovery.md` §4) are the bootstrap. |\n   327\t| low | `src/cljc/dao/stream/serving.cljc:1-11, 234-237` | \"Host-owned WebSocket serving composition\"; forwards from `:forward-anchor` (default oldest) into every accepted socket | Not wrong, but it is copy-serving. Rename or re-describe (Q7) so that \"serving\" does not imply attachment to the original stream. |\n   328\t| low | `docs/design/dao.lease.md:286-289` | \"A remote holder learns of a reclaim by observing … an ordinary `:ws/closed`. No close code is assigned here\" | With serve sessions, reclaim of a served entry is `:serve/close` with `:serve/reason`, independent of the channel's close codes; update once Q6 is decided. |\n   329\t\n   330\tThe task's read-only constraint was honored: no repository file was edited. The plan-mode plan file at `~/.claude/plans/read-collab-1790335900000-architect-dao-declarative-otter.md` records the context and verification items for this design.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '921,973p;558,579p;129,159p;536,553p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '921,973p;558,579p;129,159p;536,553p'\"","aggregated_output":"   129\trequires a host that can park at all.\n   130\t\n   131\t**Callback (asynchronous) IO.** The operation registers a continuation and\n   132\treturns at once; the runtime invokes the continuation when the answer\n   133\tarrives. Nothing waits, so one thread carries many pending operations: this\n   134\tis the model that scales. Its cost is inverted control. The caller no longer\n   135\tdecides when it observes the answer, and the inversion is viral — on a host\n   136\twithout blocking, a promise cannot be absorbed inside a function, because\n   137\tawaiting returns to the caller at the first suspension point and makes the\n   138\tcaller's own result a promise in turn. Whatever is reached from a callback\n   139\tis reached from a callback.\n   140\t\n   141\t**Non-blocking polling.** The operation returns immediately with whatever is\n   142\ttrue now, including the answer \"nothing yet, ask again.\" Nothing parks and\n   143\tnothing is inverted: the caller keeps control and decides when to ask again.\n   144\tThis is POSIX `O_NONBLOCK` without `select`, and it is DaoStream's model.\n   145\t\n   146\t`next` is total and non-blocking (see Reading): every state of the stream\n   147\thas a defined data answer, and `:dao.stream/blocked` is `EAGAIN` — this\n   148\tposition holds nothing yet. `append!`'s `:dao.stream/full` is the same\n   149\tanswer for writes. No operation in this contract parks, and none takes a\n   150\tcallback; the Result Convention's \"never exceptions and never callbacks\" is\n   151\tthis model stated as a rule about results.\n   152\t\n   153\tThe rule generalizes past `next`: **no operation waits.** An operation that\n   154\tcannot yet know its answer returns the outcome true at the moment it is\n   155\tcalled, and the eventual answer arrives the way every other fact does — as\n   156\tdata, observed later, through an ordinary operation or on a stream. A\n   157\tDaoStream operation is never a request for something to happen and be\n   158\treported back. It is a question about now.\n   159\t\n   536\t## Reading\n   537\t\n   538\t`next` takes a handle and a cursor and returns an outcome immediately.\n   539\tIt is total and non-blocking: every state of the stream has a defined data\n   540\tanswer.\n   541\t\n   542\t| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                       | Required keys                                                                           |\n   543\t|-------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n   544\t| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A value was observed.                                                         | `:dao.stream/value`, `:dao.stream/cursor` (successor)                                   |\n   545\t| `:dao.stream/blocked`         | Nothing at this position yet; retry later.                                    | —                                                                                       |\n   546\t| `:dao.stream/end`             | What this handle is on is closed and this position is past the retained tail. | —                                                                                       |\n   547\t| `:dao.stream/gap`             | This position was evicted.                                                    | `:dao.stream/cursor` (earliest retained position, or the tail when nothing is retained) |\n   548\t| `:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch` | The cursor was minted by a different logical stream.                          | —                                                                                       |\n   549\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-cursor`  | The value is not a cursor.                                                    | —                                                                                       |\n   550\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the read; nothing was observed.               | —                                                                                       |\n   551\t\n   552\tReading never mutates the stream. Two readers with cursors at the same\n   553\tposition observe the same retained value; neither can starve or affect the\n   558\t## Writing\n   559\t\n   560\t`append!` takes a handle and a value and returns an outcome:\n   561\t\n   562\t| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                                                                                              | Required keys |\n   563\t|-------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------|\n   564\t| `:dao.stream/ok`              | The value is accepted at the next position in the sequence this handle's writer surface is on. Implies neither local readability nor remote delivery. | —             |\n   565\t| `:dao.stream/full`            | The transport does not accept the value; nothing was appended. Whether this is transient or permanent is the transport's declared nature (see Retention and Gaps). | —             |\n   566\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-value`   | The transport cannot carry this value (for example, it cannot be encoded); nothing was appended.                                                     | —             |\n   567\t| `:dao.stream/closed`          | What this handle is on is closed to new appends; the value was not appended.                                                                         | —             |\n   568\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the append; nothing was appended.                                                                                    | —             |\n   569\t\n   570\tAcceptance is handle-relative, as close outcomes are. **Which sequence** a\n   571\thandle's writer surface is on is part of the transport's declared nature: for a\n   572\tring buffer it is the logical stream itself, so `ok` places the value in it; for\n   573\ta transport that carries values toward a stream elsewhere it is the ordered\n   574\toutbound path, and what becomes of the value at the far end is reported there,\n   575\tnot here. No remote transport can say more — under *no operation waits*, an\n   576\tanswer from the far end cannot arrive before `append!` returns.\n   577\t\n   578\tThe writer decides what to do about any non-`ok` outcome; DaoStream does not\n   579\tretry, buffer, or notify on its behalf.\n   921\t**Proposed resolution.** Two decisions, the first a choice:\n   922\t\n   923\t1. **Choose what a logical identity ranges over.** Either\n   924\t   (a) *one origin, one transport*: a logical stream is the sequence one\n   925\t   transport instance owns. A copy on another host or medium is a different\n   926\t   logical stream with its own identity; that it mirrors the first is a fact for an\n   927\t   interpreter to record. This is how the code already behaves:\n   928\t   `dao.stream.forward` copies into a destination with its own identity. The\n   929\t   sentence\n   930\t   about different descriptors reaching one stream narrows to \"different\n   931\t   endpoints of the same owning transport\". Or\n   932\t   (b) *contract-level position*: the contract defines a position within an\n   933\t   identity (an ordinal), cursors carry it under a `:dao.stream/…` key, and any\n   934\t   transport serving that identity honors it.\n   935\t   Recommended: (a). It matches Axiom 2 (sameness between sequences is an\n   936\t   interpretation), costs a paragraph, and leaves (b) available later as an\n   937\t   additive key. (b) commits every future transport, including ones with no\n   938\t   natural ordinal, now.\n   939\t2. **Settle serialization.** Replace \"TBD\" with: \"A cursor is plain data and\n   940\t   survives the host serialization codec structurally unchanged, under the same\n   941\t   rule as the envelopes. A cursor that outlives the stream it names yields\n   942\t   `not-found` at `attach!` or `cursor-mismatch` at `next`, never a defect.\"\n   943\t   Both existing transports already satisfy this.\n   944\t\n   945\t**Cost if deferred.** The first durable or replicated transport decides both\n   946\tquestions implicitly, by whatever its cursor happens to look like, and every\n   947\tconsumer that persisted a cursor inherits that accident.\n   948\t\n   949\t### OD-4. A logical stream can close but cannot end\n   950\t\n   951\t**The assumption.** *Complete history* obliges a transport to retain every\n   952\telement \"for as long as the logical stream exists\", and *Close* says close\n   953\t\"erases nothing\". Nothing says how a logical stream stops existing. The\n   954\tcontract assumes storage is either bounded by eviction or kept forever.\n   955\t\n   956\t**Evidence.** There is no destroy, truncate, compact, or redact anywhere in the\n   957\tsurface or in any transport. `memory-log` ends only when its process does. What\n   958\t`next`, `cursor`, or `attach!` answer for a stream whose medium was removed out\n   959\tof band is undefined; the nearest outcomes are `transport-error` and\n   960\t`not-found`, and the document chooses neither.\n   961\t\n   962\t**What breaks.** Reclaiming disk under a durable log; snapshot-and-truncate\n   963\tcompaction, the ordinary way a replayed log stays bounded; erasure that law or\n   964\ta user requires. All three are forbidden to a transport that declared complete\n   965\thistory, and consumers are wired to that declaration as a correctness\n   966\trequirement.\n   967\t\n   968\t**Proposed resolution.**\n   969\t\n   970\t1. Name a third retention nature beside *evicting* and *complete*:\n   971\t   **compacted**. A compacted transport may drop a prefix it has summarized. A\n   972\t   cursor into the dropped prefix receives `gap` exactly as under eviction, and\n   973\t   the result map may carry, under a transport-owned key, a descriptor or\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '160,330p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '160,330p'\"","aggregated_output":"   160\tThe **serve table** is composition data, not a registry: the host decides what it serves, adds and removes entries through `serve`/`unserve` transitions on its own state, and the table's lifetime is the host's business. Nothing here is namespace-global and nothing is consulted ambiently, which is the contract's \"no stream table\" preserved: a descriptor naming an identity this server does not hold is answered `:serve/disclaim`, the authoritative `not-found`.\n   161\t\n   162\tThe server is **stateless with respect to reads**. It holds no reader positions; every `next` carries its cursor. This is what lets it survive a client's silence, a duplicate request, or a session that was lost and reopened, without any resumption protocol: resumption is the client re-sending the cursor it kept.\n   163\t\n   164\tThe server never calls the owner's `close!`. A session's `:dao.stream/close!` closes only an attachment handle the server minted for that session; for a source that mints no attachments (a ring buffer, a memory log) it is a session-scoped no-op, and the session ends. Ending the served stream is its owner's act on the owner's handle, observed by the server as `end` from `next` and relayed.\n   165\t\n   166\t## 6. Channels\n   167\t\n   168\t### 6.1 Channel contract\n   169\t\n   170\tThe serve layer needs exactly what `dao.stream.ws` already provides and nothing more:\n   171\t\n   172\t1. A **writer handle** whose `append!` places one application value on an ordered (WebSocket) or unordered (UDP) outbound path, with `ok`, `full`, `invalid-value`, `closed`, `transport-error`.\n   173\t2. A **deposit-model adapter** that turns every inbound application value and every lifecycle event into one plain-data event on a medium the composition wired, correlated by an attachment identity, judging none of them.\n   174\t3. A **portable value domain** and a **frame budget** (bytes per value; unbounded for WebSocket, the datagram budget for UDP).\n   175\t4. A declaration of **ordering** (`:ordered` or `:unordered`) and **reliability** (`:reliable` or `:best-effort`), which the proxy's retry policy reads as composition data.\n   176\t\n   177\tThe channel's declared nature is what changes the proxy's policy, never its protocol.\n   178\t\n   179\t### 6.2 WebSocket channel\n   180\t\n   181\t`dao.stream.ws` is used as is, with one composition and one wording change:\n   182\t\n   183\t- **Composition.** A serve endpoint serves one path (by convention `/dao.stream.serve`) whose \"served stream\" is the channel itself: the descriptor's `:dao.stream/identity` is the serving **peer id**, not a stream. The accepted attachment's traffic medium feeds `serve-step`; the accepted socket handle is `serve-step`'s writer. `dao.stream.serving` is not used: it forwards a source into every accepted socket, which is the copy model. A small sibling composition, `dao.stream.serve.ws`, wires an accepted attachment to `serve-step` instead of to `forward-step`.\n   184\t- **Direction.** A host that dials **out** with `ws/attach!` and then runs `serve-step` over that attachment is serving. Nothing in the ws state machine cares who dialed; only its prose does (§ Contradictions, C1).\n   185\t\n   186\tHosts: clj (JDK client; server via the existing endpoint seam), cljs browser (client only; no listener exists), cljs node (both), cljd (both under `dart:io`; Flutter web is a browser).\n   187\t\n   188\t### 6.3 UDP channel\n   189\t\n   190\t`dao.stream.udp` is a new channel with the ws deposit model and no reader surface: a socket retains nothing. It is the datagram boundary the DHT node already uses, reshaped into the adapter form.\n   191\t\n   192\t- **Descriptor.** `{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/udp :dao.stream/identity <peer-id> :udp/host … :udp/port …}`. `attach!` binds a local socket (ephemeral port) and returns a handle whose writer path is \"datagrams to that address\". No handshake is needed at this layer; the serve layer's `:serve/open` is the handshake.\n   193\t- **One value per datagram**, encoded with the CBOR codec profile (binary, canonical, already specified in ws). Frame budget 1200 bytes, the DHT's figure, chosen to clear common path MTUs without fragmentation. A value over budget is `invalid-value` on `append!` and, from the server's side, `:oversize` (§3.5). IP fragmentation is not relied on; application fragmentation is deferred and, if added, lives in this channel, invisible to the serve layer.\n   194\t- **Declared nature:** `:unordered`, `:best-effort`. The proxy's retry policy therefore has real work: each outstanding request records `:sent-at`; `proxy-step` re-sends when `now - sent-at` exceeds `:retry-after-ms`, with `:max-attempts`, both composition data. Exhaustion is session loss, deposited as data.\n   195\t- **Lifecycle events** are thinner than ws: there is no peer close, so `:udp/closed` is deposited only on local close or deposit failure; peer silence is the serve layer's business (`:serve/ping` and the retry policy), not the channel's.\n   196\t- **Attachment identity** is the remote `(host, port)` pair as seen by the socket; replies go to the datagram's source address, never to a claimed address in the payload, as the DHT already rules.\n   197\t- Hosts: clj (`DatagramSocket`), cljs node (`dgram`), cljd (`RawDatagramSocket`). **Browsers have no UDP.** The browser's P2P path is a WebSocket to a relay (§7.3) today and a WebRTC DataChannel channel later (§14); both slot into §6.1 without touching the serve layer.\n   198\t\n   199\t### 6.4 A channel over a channel\n   200\t\n   201\tA relayed path is a channel whose writer wraps every value as `{:serve/frame :serve/via :serve/link l :serve/inner v}` and whose adapter unwraps `:serve/via` frames with its link from the underlying channel's medium, depositing `:serve/inner`. It declares the underlying channel's ordering and reliability. Nothing else is new: streams are values that travel through streams, and a channel is a stream. The relay itself (§7.3) sees only `:serve/via` and a link table.\n   202\t\n   203\t## 7. Reachability\n   204\t\n   205\t### 7.1 The serve descriptor\n   206\t\n   207\t```clojure\n   208\t{:dao.stream/type      :dao.stream/serve\n   209\t :dao.stream/identity  \"…\"                                   ; the served logical stream\n   210\t :serve/peer           \"…\"                                   ; the serving peer, opaque string\n   211\t :serve/surface        #{:reader}                            ; hint; :serve/opened is authoritative\n   212\t :serve/candidates     [{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/ws  :ws/host \"a.example\" :ws/port 9090 :ws/path \"/dao.stream.serve\" :dao.stream/identity \"peer-A\"}\n   213\t                        {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/udp :udp/host \"203.0.113.5\" :udp/port 4000 :dao.stream/identity \"peer-A\"}\n   214\t                        {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/udp :udp/host \"r.example\" :udp/port 4000 :dao.stream/identity \"relay-R\" :serve/via :punch :serve/peer \"peer-A\"}\n   215\t                        {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/ws  :ws/host \"r.example\" :ws/port 9090 :ws/path \"/dao.stream.serve\" :dao.stream/identity \"relay-R\" :serve/via :relay :serve/peer \"peer-A\"}]}\n   216\t```\n   217\t\n   218\tEach candidate is an ordinary channel descriptor, optionally marked `:serve/via :relay` or `:punch` with the target peer. The proxy tries candidates in order; each attempt's outcome is data on the proxy's event medium (`:serve/candidate-failed` with the index and the channel's own resolution event), and the first channel that yields `:serve/opened` is the session's. Sequential trial is a v1 simplification; parallel trial with a preference order (ICE's shape) is an additive proxy policy. The descriptor carries no authorization, as the contract requires.\n   219\t\n   220\t### 7.2 Direct\n   221\t\n   222\tThe serving host listens; the candidate is a plain ws or udp descriptor. This is the existing ws model and the only one `dao.stream.ws.md` describes.\n   223\t\n   224\t### 7.3 Relay: outbound-only serving\n   225\t\n   226\tA host behind NAT dials a **relay** R (any host with a reachable address running `relay-step`) and sends `:serve/register` with its peer id, then keeps the channel alive with `:serve/ping`. A client dials R and sends `:serve/dial` naming the peer and a link id it minted. R's state is `{:peers {peer-id → channel-writer} :links {link → [writer-a writer-b]}}`; on `:serve/dial` it pairs the link with the registered peer's channel and thereafter forwards `:serve/via` frames between the two writers by link, copying and judging nothing. Both sides then run the serve protocol over the §6.4 channel. R never sees a session or an operation. A relay is a forwarder: its step is `forward-step` with a link-keyed destination.\n   227\t\n   228\tThis is the browser's whole NAT story and every host's fallback. Its costs are honest: the relay carries every byte, and a registered peer is reachable only while its outbound channel lives, so `:serve/ping` cadence is bounded by NAT binding lifetime (tens of seconds for UDP, longer for TCP). A relay is composition, not infrastructure: anyone runs one, and `dao.stream.discovery`'s rendezvous topics are how a peer finds one.\n   229\t\n   230\t### 7.4 Rendezvous and UDP hole punching\n   231\t\n   232\tBoth peers behind NAT, both with UDP. Each registers with a rendezvous S over UDP; S records the **reflexive address** (source host and port as S observed them) per peer. On `:serve/dial`, S sends `:serve/introduce` to both peers carrying the other's reflexive address and the link. Each peer then sends `:serve/punch` probes with the link's nonce to the other's reflexive address on a schedule `proxy-step` reads from `now`; the first probe to arrive opens the NAT binding in the other direction, and the first `:serve/punch` received is answered with a `:serve/pong`. When both sides have a pong the direct udp channel exists and the serve session opens over it. If no pong arrives within the punch budget, the proxy falls to the next candidate, normally the relay through the same S (a rendezvous is also a relay, and one channel to it serves both roles).\n   233\t\n   234\tThis is STUN and ICE reduced to what the datom.world model needs: reflexive addresses are facts deposited by S, probes are values, and the state machine is a step. Symmetric NATs that assign a fresh port per destination defeat punching and take the relay path; that is a property of the network, reported as a failed candidate, not a failure of the protocol.\n   235\t\n   236\t### 7.5 P2P symmetry\n   237\t\n   238\tOnce any channel exists between A and B, each may `:serve/open` sessions on the other's serve table, in both directions, on the same channel. There is no client host and server host, only per-session roles. A continuation migrating from A to B whose cells reference streams on A is served by A over the very channel B dialed to fetch it. This falls out of §3.1 and needs no additional design.\n   239\t\n   240\t### 7.6 Liveness\n   241\t\n   242\t`:serve/ping` and `:serve/pong` are per channel. Cadence and the number of unanswered pings that end a channel are composition data in the driver's clock domain. A channel ended by silence deposits its lifecycle event; every session on it is lost (§4). This resolves the ws document's deferred *Liveness* item for channels used by this layer, with ordinary values rather than protocol frames, because a protocol frame would be a fact deposited by nobody.\n   243\t\n   244\t## 8. Host isolation\n   245\t\n   246\t| Piece                                  | clj | cljs browser | cljs node | cljd | Where the host code lives |\n   247\t|----------------------------------------|-----|--------------|-----------|------|---------------------------|\n   248\t| serve frames, proxy, server, relay, rendezvous steps | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | `.cljc`, pure data, no host code |\n   249\t| ws channel, dial out                   | ✓   | ✓            | ✓         | ✓    | existing `:connect!` seams |\n   250\t| ws channel, listen                     | ✓   | —            | ✓         | ✓    | existing endpoint seam |\n   251\t| udp channel                            | ✓   | —            | ✓         | ✓    | new socket seam, same shape as ws's `:send!`/`:close!` |\n   252\t| relayed channel (§6.4)                 | ✓   | ✓            | ✓         | ✓    | `.cljc` |\n   253\t| hole punching                          | ✓   | —            | ✓         | ✓    | `.cljc` over the udp seam |\n   254\t| WebRTC DataChannel (deferred)          | —   | later        | —         | later (Flutter) | a future channel |\n   255\t\n   256\tA browser peer therefore serves and proxies through a relay; a browser can be a P2P participant behind NAT, just not a hole-punching one until a DataChannel channel exists.\n   257\t\n   258\t## 9. UCF integration\n   259\t\n   260\tWhat §7.4.3 and §7.5.3 should say, replacing the undefined \"standard stream-over-network facade\":\n   261\t\n   262\t- **Lift.** For each stream cell, the exporter checks: the handle's declared surface covers what the cell needs; its cursors and outcome maps are in the portable domain (`:dao.stream.serve/v1`, the one cursor profile); and the exporter has at least one reachability candidate. It adds `identity → {handle, surface}` to its serve table and writes a serve descriptor into the cell's `:yin.k/stream`. The cell's `:yin.k/position` is the kept cursor, verbatim. Any check failing is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` before minting, as the UCF already says.\n   263\t- **Lower.** The resumer `attach!`s each serve descriptor, receiving a proxy. `next` at the kept cursor is the first demand; `blocked` until served; the source's `gap` arrives verbatim. `:serve/disclaim` on open is `not-found` and hence `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the identity. A candidate list that is exhausted is the same.\n   264\t- **Lifetime.** The exporter must keep the entry served while the migrated task may need it. The correct home for that obligation is a lease (`dao.lease.md`): the served entry is a grant, the resumer renews, and lapse unserves. Until that lease exists, the exporter keeps the entry until its own composition retires it, and a resumer that arrives after retirement gets `not-found`. This is stated as an open question (Q6) rather than designed here.\n   265\t- **Pinning.** Serving from the emitter pins the emitter: the stream is where it is, and OD-3(a) makes a copy a different stream. Re-homing a stream is out of scope and should be said so in the UCF.\n   266\t\n   267\t## 10. Invariant compliance\n   268\t\n   269\t- **No hidden global state.** Serve tables, sessions, links, peers and windows are values inside compositions the host constructs and drives; nothing is namespace-global and no operation consults ambient state.\n   270\t- **No implicit control flow.** Every step is called by a driver with `now`; the only callback code is the channel adapter, which deposits and returns, as the ws adapter does today.\n   271\t- **No raw callbacks.** Resolution, detachment, append outcomes, candidate failures and reflexive addresses are all deposited events, read by cursors.\n   272\t- **No shared mutable state.** A proxy's window and a server's session record are owned by exactly one driver; the source handle is touched only by `serve-step`.\n   273\t- **No layer collapsing.** Relay and rendezvous interpret nothing; the channel interprets nothing; the serve layer interprets operations but never values; the reader interprets values.\n   274\t- **No assumed graphs.** Who serves what to whom is the serve table and the descriptor's candidates, explicit data, never a topology the protocol infers.\n   275\t\n   276\t## 11. Concurrency and linearization\n   277\t\n   278\tThe source is operated only from `serve-step`, in the order requests are dequeued. Reads carry their own cursors, so concurrent readers through one or many proxies neither share nor contend for position. Writes are linearized per session by the one-outstanding rule, and across sessions by the source's own sequence; the contract promises nothing more for concurrent writers and neither does this layer. Over an unordered channel the proxy never issues an `append!` before every earlier request on that session has been answered, so a `:newest` mint that must precede an append does precede it at the source. The relay preserves per-link order only if its underlying channel does; a relayed UDP link is unordered and the proxy's policy already accounts for that through the channel's declaration.\n   279\t\n   280\t## 12. What is contract, transport-owned and deferred\n   281\t\n   282\t- **The contract requires** (and this design depends on): OD-3 resolutions (a) and (2), cursors as plain data that survive serialization; OD-2's payload-correlation rule; OD-1's unrecognized-outcome rule with `:dao.stream/retry?`. None of the three is accepted yet (see C3).\n   283\t- **Transport-owned by this layer:** the frame vocabulary, session identity, the one-outstanding-append rule, anchor piggybacking, held reads, candidate order, retry and reconnect policies, relay and rendezvous state.\n   284\t- **Deliberately deferred:** UDP application fragmentation; parallel candidate trial; WebRTC DataChannel; authorization and postage for `:serve/register` and `:serve/dial` (a relay is a spam surface, and the contract's no-gating stance plus `dao.stream.discovery`'s postage note apply); self-certifying peer ids (v1 peer ids are opaque strings the composition assigns; the discovery document's kickoff-hash identities slot in as an additive rule); a lease over served entries; encryption of the channel (WSS is the host's; UDP has none until a Noise-shaped channel exists, and until then UDP serving is for trusted networks, as the DHT already says).\n   285\t\n   286\t## 13. Completion criteria\n   287\t\n   288\t1. **Loopback conformance.** Serve a memory log and a ring buffer through an in-process channel pair (two ring buffers as the two directions). The proxy passes the contract's reader and writer tests, and every cursor the proxy hands out is structurally equal to one the source minted.\n   289\t2. **Gap fidelity.** Evict at the source under a kept cursor; the proxy relays `gap` with the source's recovery cursor, with no replay and no skip.\n   290\t3. **Anchor semantics.** A `:newest` minted through the proxy never skips a value appended after the mint.\n   291\t4. **UDP under loss, reorder and duplication** (a lossy socket seam): no duplicate append at the source, no lost read, no proxy-fabricated gap, and `:serve/append-unknown` deposited when the session dies with an append in flight.\n   292\t5. **Outbound serving.** A peer that only dials out serves a stream to a peer that dialed the same relay; the relay's state never holds a session.\n   293\t6. **Punch and fallback.** Two UDP peers behind a NAT simulator open a direct channel through a rendezvous; with a symmetric-NAT simulator they fall to the relay candidate, reported as data.\n   294\t7. **P2P symmetry.** Two peers each serve one stream to the other over one channel.\n   295\t8. **UCF §7.11 facade acceptance**, as written there: park with a string-backed stream at a kept cursor, migrate, resume, force eviction, observe `gap`; a non-portable source refuses at lift; an unreachable endpoint refuses at lower.\n   296\t9. **Portability.** Items 1–4 and 7 run on clj, cljs node and cljd; item 5 also on cljs browser through the ws relay path.\n   297\t\n   298\t## 14. Deferred\n   299\t\n   300\tListed in §12. Additionally: a copy-serving mode remains useful (broadcast to many readers with local retention) and is what `dao.stream.serving` already is; it should be renamed or re-described so that \"serving\" no longer suggests attachment to the original stream (C2).\n   301\t\n   302\t---\n   303\t\n   304\t# Open questions for the owner\n   305\t\n   306\t- **Q1. Append through a proxy.** As designed, `append!` on a proxy answers `ok` for acceptance into the session's outbound path and the source's outcome arrives later on the event medium, as ws does and the contract's Writing section allows. The UCF's `:put` rule (\"retries its retained value and resumes on `ok`\") would then resume a program on an append the source may still refuse. Alternative: the proxy answers `full` until the source's outcome arrives and then reports that outcome, holding the value meanwhile, which gives the writer the source's truth from `append!` itself at the cost of a proxy-level convention (one held value, writer retries the same value). Recommendation: keep the contract-consistent design and amend UCF §7.4.3 so that a `:put` on a served stream resumes when the source's outcome is observed, not on outbound `ok`.\n   307\t- **Q2. Held reads in v1.** They cut network chatter for `blocked`-heavy readers such as a parked VM polling a response cell, at the cost of per-session server state and a hold budget. Recommendation: include, since the UCF's dominant pattern is exactly a kept response cursor polled until a value lands.\n   308\t- **Q3. Peer identity.** Opaque composition-assigned strings now, self-certifying kickoff-hash identities later, or self-certifying from the start. Recommendation: opaque now; the relay routes by string equality either way, and signature verification is an additive rule on `:serve/register`.\n   309\t- **Q4. Relay and rendezvous gating.** v1 accepts any `:serve/register` and `:serve/dial`. Recommendation: accept for the dev-only repo, record postage as the intended answer.\n   310\t- **Q5. Accept OD-1, OD-2 and OD-3 (a)+(2) now.** This design is not implementable without OD-3 and is dishonest without OD-2; OD-1 is needed only for the `:establishing` answer. Recommendation: accept all three as a precondition of the implementation phase.\n   311\t- **Q6. Lifetime of a served entry after migration.** Lease-governed (recommended, via `dao.lease`), or composition-retired with `not-found` as the honest late answer.\n   312\t- **Q7. Naming.** `dao.stream.serve` for this layer and a rename of today's `dao.stream.serving` to `dao.stream.serving.copy` or similar, so the two models are not confused.\n   313\t\n   314\t# Contradictions found\n   315\t\n   316\t| Severity | File:line | Evidence | Recommended correction |\n   317\t|---|---|---|---|\n   318\t| high | `docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:18-26` | \"serving is what makes any stream remotely attachable … remote interpreters can attach\" | Only true of copies: the client reads a local medium with local positions (OD-3 evidence, `dao.stream.md:906-911`). Reword to: the ws transport serves copies; attaching to the original stream is `dao.stream.serve`. |\n   319\t| high | `docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:9-16, 455-459` | descriptor \"names a server-hosted stream\"; endpoint constructor is bind-host/advertised-host only; no outbound-serving mode | Add: direction of establishment is independent of the serving role; a dialed-out attachment may carry `serve-step`. The state machine already permits it; only the prose forbids it. |\n   320\t| high | `docs/design/dao.stream.md:523-525` (OD-3), `:851-894` (OD-2), `:811-849` (OD-1) | \"Whether a cursor survives serialization to another host is transport-owned and TBD\"; append's third state undefined; unrecognized outcome undefined | This design requires OD-3 (a)+(2) and OD-2, and uses OD-1's `:dao.stream/retry?`. Accept them; move the text into the sections they amend. |\n   321\t| high | `docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:520-534` | \"standard stream-over-network facade derived from the dao.stream operation and outcome contract … `dao.stream.rpc.ws` demonstrates … does not itself supply the UCF facade\" | Cite `dao.stream.serve.md`; the facade is the serve protocol, the proxy is its handle, and the exporter's obligation is §9 of this design. |\n   322\t| medium | `docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:554-556` | \"a `:put` wait retries its retained value and resumes with it on `ok`\" | Through a proxy, `ok` is outbound acceptance. Resume on the observed source outcome (Q1), or adopt the alternative and say so. |\n   323\t| medium | `docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:780, 929` | `:yin.k/cursor-profiles #{:dao.stream/file-v1}` — a per-transport profile set | One profile, `:dao.stream.serve/v1` (cursor and outcomes in the portable domain). Per-transport profiles would make the resumer know transports, which the proxy exists to prevent. |\n   324\t| medium | `docs/design/daostream-udp-design.md:199-284, 470` | DRDS sequence numbers, ACK vectors, sliding window; \"NAT traversal (STUN / ICE)\" as deferred | Superseded: serve reads are idempotent by cursor, so no DRDS; a datagram stream with its own positions is a different logical stream under OD-3(a). Mark the document superseded prior art; NAT moves to this spec. |\n   325\t| medium | `docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:692-697` | Liveness and resumption protocol listed as deferred | Resumption is answered by kept cursors plus a stateless server (§5); liveness by `:serve/ping` at the serve layer (§7.6). Point the deferred items here. |\n   326\t| low | `docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:165-167` | \"NAT traversal is unaddressed … hole-punching, relay/TURN, and bootstrap discovery\" | Cite §7.3–7.4; a DHT node is a natural rendezvous host and rendezvous topics (`dao.stream.discovery.md` §4) are the bootstrap. |\n   327\t| low | `src/cljc/dao/stream/serving.cljc:1-11, 234-237` | \"Host-owned WebSocket serving composition\"; forwards from `:forward-anchor` (default oldest) into every accepted socket | Not wrong, but it is copy-serving. Rename or re-describe (Q7) so that \"serving\" does not imply attachment to the original stream. |\n   328\t| low | `docs/design/dao.lease.md:286-289` | \"A remote holder learns of a reclaim by observing … an ordinary `:ws/closed`. No close code is assigned here\" | With serve sessions, reclaim of a served entry is `:serve/close` with `:serve/reason`, independent of the channel's close codes; update once Q6 is decided. |\n   329\t\n   330\tThe task's read-only constraint was honored: no repository file was edited. The plan-mode plan file at `~/.claude/plans/read-collab-1790335900000-architect-dao-declarative-otter.md` records the context and verification items for this design.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
