{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '472,558p;669,744p'\"","aggregated_output":"   472\t### 7.4.3 The pending wait travels as data — complete, and resumable elsewhere\n   473\t\n   474\tA parked machine was waiting for something. That something is a resource of\n   475\tthe emitter's host, and the resumer cannot poll it. `:yin.k/pending` records\n   476\tthe wait in resource-independent terms so the resumer can re-establish an\n   477\t*equivalent* wait, or decide it cannot. The first draft carried sketches;\n   478\tthe set here is exhaustive over §7.4.1's reasons, and each variant carries\n   479\teverything a *foreign* resumer needs — including the state the first draft\n   480\tleft implicit on the emitter's heap:\n   481\t\n   482\t```clojure\n   483\t;; blocked read (:stream-next)\n   484\t{:yin.k/reason   :next\n   485\t :yin.k/cell     :yin.k/c-17}          ; the cursor CELL id, §7.5.3 — not a bare position\n   486\t\n   487\t;; blocked write (:stream-put, or an effectful call's :stream/put)\n   488\t{:yin.k/reason   :put\n   489\t :yin.k/stream   {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}   ; the target\n   490\t :yin.k/value    <encoded>}            ; the retained value — a retry appends THIS, and\n   491\t                                       ; without it the wait is not re-establishable\n   492\t\n   493\t;; FFI call, sent (:ffi-call → ok)\n   494\t{:yin.k/reason     :ffi\n   495\t :yin.k/call-id    :call-7             ; the :dao.stream.apply/id correlation\n   496\t :yin.k/op         :op/add\n   497\t :yin.k/request    {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}   ; where the request went\n   498\t :yin.k/response   {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}   ; where the answer lands\n   499\t :yin.k/cell       :yin.k/c-23         ; the response cursor cell, §7.5.3, at its KEPT position}\n   500\t\n   501\t;; FFI call, retained (:ffi-call → full): the request is still in hand\n   502\t{:yin.k/reason     :ffi-request\n   503\t :yin.k/call-id    :call-7\n   504\t :yin.k/request-op :op/add\n   505\t :yin.k/request-args [<encoded> …]     ; enough to rebuild the envelope verbatim\n   506\t :yin.k/request    {identity/descriptor as above}\n   507\t :yin.k/response   {identity/descriptor as above}\n   508\t :yin.k/cell       :yin.k/c-23}        ; response cell at its kept position\n   509\t```\n   510\t\n   511\tThree rules make these resumable somewhere other than where they were minted:\n   512\t\n   513\t- **The response position is a kept cursor, never an anchor.** Reattaching\n   514\t  at `:oldest` replays responses that belong to other calls; attaching at\n   515\t  `:newest` skips past this call's own. The response cell therefore travels\n   516\t  at its *kept* position, and the whole task's waiters on that cell travel\n   517\t  with it (§7.6.3), so the round-order semantics of `check-wait-set` —\n   518\t  waiters sharing a cell consume successive values — are preserved on the\n   519\t  resumer exactly. A first poll that observes `gap` is the honest outcome\n   520\t  the contract already promises a kept cursor (`dao.stream.md`, *Retention\n   521\t  and Gaps*). At migration the composition serves every carried stream\n   522\t  cell through a standard stream-over-network facade derived from the\n   523\t  `dao.stream` operation and outcome contract. This is an access path to\n   524\t  the original logical stream, not a copied stream with new positions.\n   525\t  `dao.stream` itself assumes no network. The facade serves the source's\n   526\t  declared reader and writer surface, preserving its outcome maps and\n   527\t  cursor namespace rather than assigning new positions.\n   528\t  The cell's stream descriptor carries the generated facade endpoint,\n   529\t  while the cell carries its kept cursor. The receiver attaches a proxy\n   530\t  `dao.stream` implementation through that endpoint and resumes at the\n   531\t  kept cursor. `dao.stream.rpc.ws` demonstrates the transport-neutral RPC\n   532\t  pattern over ring buffers; it does not itself supply the UCF facade.\n   533\t  `:yin.k/cursor-profiles` in `:yin.k/requires` declares what the facade\n   534\t  must honor, including cursor identity, position, and `gap` outcomes.\n   535\t  If the source cannot expose those operations, preserve the cursor, or\n   536\t  keep the endpoint reachable, lift refuses as `:yin.k/unsatisfied` before\n   537\t  minting the value. Attach failure on the receiver is also\n   538\t  `:yin.k/unsatisfied`, naming the stream identity.\n   539\t- **Outstanding calls route to the emitter's pair; the resumer's pair is its\n   540\t  own.** The reference machine restores a `:request-sent` entry into a\n   541\t  response wait through its fixed local store keys (`vm/call-out-stream-key`\n   542\t  and friends). A resumer must *not* do that: the outstanding call's\n   543\t  response will arrive on the **emitter's** response stream, because that is\n   544\t  where the request was sent. The resumer re-establishes the wait against\n   545\t  the carried response descriptor and the cell's fresh local key (the cell\n   546\t  id remapped per §7.5.3), while calls the resumed program makes *after*\n   547\t  lowering use the resumer's own pair under its own keys. Pending-call\n   548\t  routing and future-call routing are separate stores of state; conflating\n   549\t  them strands the migrated call exactly as the review described.\n   550\t- **On resume, the resumer attaches and re-mints.** Attach to every\n   551\t  descriptor in the frame, pending, and cells; an attachment resolving to\n   552\t  `not-found` is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream identity, never a\n   553\t  silent `nil`; a `:put` wait retries its retained value and resumes with it\n   554\t  on `ok` (the engine, a woken writer's retry appends and stamps the\n   555\t  value); a `:next` wait polls its cell. No wait is silently dropped and no\n   556\t  retained value is recomputed — what parks is what resumes.\n   557\t\n   558\t## §7.5 Recursive portable encoding (blocker 2)\n   669\t### 7.5.3 Sharing, cells, and cycles\n   670\t\n   671\t**The value table.** A configuration is a DAG, not a tree: a closure's\n   672\tcaptured environment is the frame's environment; a value on the stack is\n   673\talso bound in `E`. Encoding naively duplicates, and a deep K makes\n   674\tduplication quadratic. The encoder therefore builds a **value table**:\n   675\t\n   676\t- On encountering a collection, closure, or continuation it has already\n   677\t  encoded (host identity during one lift), it emits\n   678\t  `{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/ref :yin.k/id addr}` where `addr` is the `dao.jing`\n   679\t  address of the encoded subvalue, and places the subvalue in\n   680\t  `:yin.k/values {addr → encoded}`.\n   681\t- A subvalue is placed in the table when it is referenced more than once or\n   682\t  its encoded size exceeds a composition-supplied threshold; otherwise it\n   683\t  stays inline. Both choices yield equal semantics; only the table is needed\n   684\t  for correctness of sharing.\n   685\t- A cycle (a value reachable from itself through host identity) is refused\n   686\t  as `:yin.k/non-portable` with `:yin.k/kind :cyclic` naming the path. The\n   687\t  reference machine cannot produce one — `E` is extended by `merge`, never\n   688\t  mutated — so the refusal guards against foreign engines and bridge-returned\n   689\t  host structures. Cycles *through ref data* cannot be minted at all and are\n   690\t  rejected at decode (§7.5.1); this single representation retires the first\n   691\t  draft's two competing ones.\n   692\t\n   693\tBecause table entries are content-addressed, a carrier that already holds an\n   694\tentry (a `dao.jing` intake pool, a DHT replica) need not carry it again, and\n   695\ttwo continuations parked from the same closure share its encoding. This is\n   696\t`streams-all-the-way-down.md` §6.3's checkpoint-as-published-fold applied to\n   697\ta single frame. It is also why §7.3.4 separates `:yin.k/id` (which varies\n   698\twith these choices, harmlessly) from `:yin.k/occurrence` (which does not).\n   699\t\n   700\t**Cursor cells.** A cursor is *observable state with aliasing*: two distinct\n   701\tcursor refs may sit at the same position and yet be independently\n   702\tadvanceable store entries, while repeated refs to one cursor ref must\n   703\tadvance together — `check-wait-set` advances a shared cursor ref before\n   704\tpolling the next waiter, so waiters on one ref read successive values, and\n   705\twaiters on two refs at the same position read the same next value\n   706\t(`yin.vm.engine`, `check-wait-set`). A position-only encoding erases\n   707\texactly this distinction: two cells at one position would collapse, and\n   708\ttheir independent advancement would be lost. The encoding therefore carries\n   709\t**logical cursor cells** separately from position content:\n   710\t\n   711\t```clojure\n   712\t:yin.k/cells {:yin.k/c-17 {:yin.k/stream   {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/stream …}\n   713\t                           :yin.k/position <opaque, exactly as the transport minted it>}}\n   714\t;; values reference cells, never positions:\n   715\t{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/cursor-ref :yin.k/cell :yin.k/c-17}\n   716\t```\n   717\t\n   718\tCell ids are minted by the emitter (stable within one lift; remapped on\n   719\tlowering) and are *not* content-addressed — a cell is a local identity, like\n   720\ta tempid, and deduplicating cells by their contents is precisely the aliasing\n   721\terasure this structure exists to prevent. On lowering, each cell becomes a\n   722\tfresh store cursor entry seeded with its carried position; every\n   723\t`:yin.k/cursor-ref` to it remaps to that entry; two refs to one cell share\n   724\tone entry and one ref per cell keeps its own. The FFI response cell of\n   725\t§7.4.3 is a cell like any other.\n   726\t\n   727\tA stream reference is portable when the exporter can serve its declared\n   728\t`dao.stream` surface through the facade and publish an attachable endpoint\n   729\tfor the original logical stream. This applies to local implementations,\n   730\tincluding an in-memory or string-backed stream; they need no native\n   731\tnetwork transport. The exporter checks the required cursor profile before\n   732\tencoding the cell, and the receiver binds a proxy on `attach!`. The facade\n   733\tpreserves the source's cursor and outcome semantics, including `gap`; it\n   734\tdoes not turn `:oldest` or `:newest` into a kept position. A raw host handle\n   735\tstill never crosses the value boundary. A stream that cannot be served or\n   736\tcannot honor the required profile refuses through the existing\n   737\tnon-portable or unsatisfied outcomes; a missing remote attachment is\n   738\t`not-found` and becomes `:yin.k/unsatisfied`.\n   739\t\n   740\t### 7.5.4 Failure is total and names its place\n   741\t\n   742\tEncoding a continuation with one refused leaf refuses the whole\n   743\tcontinuation:\n   744\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md | sed -n '7,25p;32,47p;336,372p;550,590p;690,706p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md | sed -n '7,25p;32,47p;336,372p;550,590p;690,706p'\"","aggregated_output":"     7\t## What the Descriptor Names\n     8\t\n     9\tA WebSocket descriptor names a **server-hosted stream**, never a connection.\n    10\tThe stream exists on the serving host — created there, retained there,\n    11\tclosed there by its owner — and it exists whether or not any client is\n    12\tcurrently connected. A connection is the mechanics of one attachment:\n    13\tconnecting joins the stream, disconnecting detaches from it, and neither\n    14\tevent creates, closes, or destroys anything. That is what makes `attach!`\n    15\thonest here: the same descriptor reaches the same stream every time, because\n    16\tthe stream's identity and lifecycle belong to the server, not to the wire.\n    17\t\n    18\tThis framing generalizes: **serving is what makes any stream remotely\n    19\tattachable.** A ring buffer is host-local only because nothing speaks for\n    20\tit. A host that composes a WebSocket endpoint in front of one is serving\n    21\tthat ring buffer; the endpoint produces a descriptor carrying its address and\n    22\tthe ring buffer's unchanged logical-stream identity, and remote\n    23\tinterpreters can attach. The transport owns no stream — it serves streams\n    24\tthe host composed, which is exactly the contract's rule that descriptors\n    25\tresolve against \"the streams a network endpoint serves because serving them\n    32\t## Surfaces\n    33\t\n    34\tA WebSocket handle is **writer** and **closable**, plus `descriptor` as every\n    35\thandle. It has no reader surface: a socket retains nothing, so it cannot make\n    36\tthe reader surface's promise — a positioned, append-only, retained sequence\n    37\tthat cursors can observe and re-observe. `cursor` and `next` do not exist on it.\n    38\t\n    39\tBoth a client handle and a server-side accepted-connection handle answer\n    40\t`descriptor` with reachability for the endpoint through which they attached\n    41\tand with the served stream's transport-independent `:dao.stream/identity`.\n    42\tTwo endpoints may therefore produce different descriptors for the same served\n    43\tstream, while every such result carries the same identity.\n    44\t`:dao.stream/attachment` distinguishes connections; neither the descriptor nor\n    45\tthe logical-stream identity does.\n    46\t\n    47\tThe writer surface is on the connection's **ordered outbound path** — the\n   336\t### Ending a served stream\n   337\t\n   338\tClosing a served stream is its owner's `close!`, and connected clients have to\n   339\tbe able to tell that from a connection that merely dropped. The two call for\n   340\topposite responses — a dropped connection means reattach, an ended stream\n   341\tmeans stop — and a client has no reader surface, so the answer can only reach\n   342\tit as a deposited event.\n   343\t\n   344\tThe closing frame carries it. When the serving host closes a stream it closes\n   345\teach attached connection **with a distinguishing close code**, rather than\n   346\tdropping the socket. The client's adapter reads that code and deposits\n   347\t`:ws/ended` instead of `:ws/closed`. (The exact code is `4000`, mapping to\n   348\t`:ws/ended`, while `4002` is protocol-error and `4004` is the authoritative\n   349\tnot-found disclaimer; see Elements and Serialization.)\n   350\t\n   351\tWhat separates the two cases is not the closing but whether anything was\n   352\theard. A connection lost to a network fault delivers no closing frame at all,\n   353\tand the host reports that distinctly — the client learns that nobody told it\n   354\tanything, and deposits `:ws/closed`.\n   355\t\n   356\tThree outcomes follow, and the third is the honest limit:\n   357\t\n   358\t- **A closing frame arrived** — the stream ended. Stop; there is nothing to\n   359\t  reattach to.\n   360\t- **Nothing arrived** — reattach. If the stream is in fact gone, the handshake\n   361\t  answers with the authoritative disclaimer and the client learns on that\n   362\t  attempt instead of this one. A frame lost in transit costs one wasted\n   363\t  reattach, never a wrong conclusion the client cannot escape.\n   364\t- **The server cannot be reached at all** — unknowable. Nothing can distinguish\n   365\t  a crashed peer from an unreachable one, so a client keeps retrying, which is\n   366\t  correct: the stream may still be there. Bounding those retries is the\n   367\t  consumer's policy, not this transport's.\n   368\t\n   369\tThe close code is the fast path for the common case — a healthy server ending a\n   370\tstream deliberately. The handshake's disclaimer is what makes every other case\n   371\tresolve as soon as a server is reachable. Neither is sufficient alone.\n   372\t\n   550\t  vocabulary, and exactly one resolution event is deposited per attachment:\n   551\t  `:ws/opened` when the endpoint accepted the stream, and otherwise one of the\n   552\t  two failures. `:ws/not-found` is the authoritative answer that what the\n   553\t  descriptor names does not exist — the endpoint responded and disclaimed\n   554\t  the stream — and is not retried automatically (though a server may serve\n   555\t  that identity later; retrying on external evidence is interpreter\n   556\t  policy), while `:ws/transport-error` covers transient\n   557\t  reachability and connection failures — DNS, timeout, routing, refusal —\n   558\t  and retrying it may succeed on its own. A caller's retry policy is as\n   559\t  deterministic as before; it reads the answer from the medium instead of\n   560\t  from a return value.\n   561\t- `append!` returns `:dao.stream/full` when the send buffer cannot accept\n   562\t  the value now — **transient**: the network drains the buffer on its own,\n   563\t  and a later append succeeds. An attachment is establishing until the client\n   564\t  has **received** `:ws/accept` (or, server-side, until `endpoint-step` has sent\n   565\t  it and enabled delivery); throughout that interval `append!` returns `full`\n   566\t  and sends nothing.\n   567\t  `:dao.stream/closed` once the connection is down, which includes an\n   568\t  attachment whose connection never succeeded. A value that cannot be\n   569\t  encoded for the wire is `:dao.stream/invalid-value`. A local send that\n   570\t  fails for neither reason is `:dao.stream/transport-error`. Operating the\n   571\t  handle therefore tells a caller **that** the attachment is not writable,\n   572\t  needing no reader at all — but never why: an authoritative `not-found` and\n   573\t  a transient failure both reduce to `closed` here, and the distinction\n   574\t  survives only as the correlated **resolution** event on the deposit medium\n   575\t  — never as a lifecycle event, which is precisely the class that does not\n   576\t  carry it.\n   577\t- `close!` on an attached handle means **disconnect from the server**: the\n   578\t  handle is on the attachment, so closing it ends the connection,\n   579\t  idempotently, and this handle's subsequent `append!` returns\n   580\t  `:dao.stream/closed`. The served stream is untouched — closing *it*\n   581\t  belongs to its server-side owner. Disconnection is not final: the\n   582\t  descriptor remains valid and `attach!` rejoins the same stream. The\n   583\t  close-completion callback at each endpoint deposits the departure into its\n   584\t  host-composed event medium, correlated by attachment identity. If releasing\n   585\t  the host socket fails after the local state transition, the local traffic\n   586\t  medium first receives\n   587\t  `{:ws/attachment <id> :ws/event :ws/error :ws/reason :ws/close-failure}`;\n   588\t  the later close-completion still contributes the single terminal lifecycle\n   589\t  event. A diagnostic deposit that itself fails follows Deposit Admission.\n   590\t- Connection loss is the same detachment, uninvited: the served stream\n   690\t- Exact envelope key set (tracks the contract's descriptor TBD).\n   691\t- Liveness: whether an idle connection is probed with the protocol's own\n   692\t  ping/pong frames or with ordinary deposited values, how often, how many\n   693\t  unanswered probes end a connection, and — if the probe is not a protocol\n   694\t  frame — which component is obliged to answer it.\n   695\t- Resumption protocol: stream identity is stable across reconnects, so\n   696\t  resumption is possible by design; what history a rejoining client receives\n   697\t  (and how it states where it left off) is not yet specified.\n   698\t- Backpressure protocols above the stream (a reader publishing its progress\n   699\t  as data is interpretation, outside both this transport and the contract).\n   700\t  Needing nothing from this transport does not mean needing no specification:\n   701\t  a pause vocabulary both ends must recognize is interoperable wire content,\n   702\t  and it requires a durable home before it is built. `dao.lease.md` is the\n   703\t  intended home for its semantics and does not yet claim them; the wire form\n   704\t  is owed by neither that document nor this one until it does. The v2\n   705\t  migration plan has deferred flow control out of its slice for the same\n   706\t  reason, so nothing is being built against an unsettled vocabulary.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"agent_message","text":"The main coupling is between Q1 and Q5: the proxy’s outbound `ok` is not the source’s append result, and the UCF currently resumes a `:put` on `ok`. I’m checking the proposed OD resolutions and migration rules before settling that position."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '69,115p;120,159p;202,280p;313,351p;473,553p;558,579p;921,955p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '69,115p;120,159p;202,280p;313,351p;473,553p;558,579p;921,955p'\"","aggregated_output":"    69\t## Invariants\n    70\t\n    71\t- Stream elements are data. DaoStream assigns them no intrinsic meaning.\n    72\t- Reading is non-destructive. A reader advances its own cursor, not the stream.\n    73\t- Multiple cursors may observe the same retained value independently.\n    74\t- Interpreters own cursors and decide when to call `next`.\n    75\t- A runtime may drive an interpreter, but DaoStream does not require a runtime.\n    76\t- Closing a stream changes future availability but does not itself erase\n    77\t  retained history.\n    78\t- Retention may be bounded. A cursor over evicted history receives a gap\n    79\t  outcome as data; the reader decides what missing data means.\n    80\t- All expected operational outcomes are data, not exceptions or callbacks.\n    81\t- No operation waits. Every operation returns what is true at the moment it is\n    82\t  called; an answer not yet knowable arrives later as data.\n    83\t- No hidden global state. DaoStream keeps no registry of streams, handles, or\n    84\t  observers, and no operation consults ambient state.\n    85\t- A logical stream has an identity that is plain data, the same through every\n    86\t  handle on it, stable across attachment lifecycles and across serialization.\n    87\t  Projecting that identity never fails.\n    88\t\n    89\tEvery clause below must be consistent with these invariants, and most derive\n    90\tfrom one directly. Where a clause is instead an interoperability decision — the\n    91\tdescriptor's properties, the result keyword namespace, the anchor set — it is\n    92\tstated as a decision rather than dressed as a deduction. A proposed addition\n    93\tthat contradicts an invariant does not belong in this contract; one that merely\n    94\tcannot be deduced from them must say which decision it is and why.\n    95\t\n    96\t## Result Convention\n    97\t\n    98\tEvery DaoStream operation returns data.\n    99\t\n   100\t- Each operation's set of outcomes is **exhaustive**: a transport may produce\n   101\t  fewer of them, never any outside the set. Which subset it produces is part of\n   102\t  its declared nature (see Surfaces). An outcome is a map whose\n   103\t  `:dao.stream/outcome` key names the outcome with a qualified keyword. Every\n   104\t  operation returns such a map — including projections like `descriptor` and\n   105\t  `cursor`, whose success map carries the projected value.\n   106\t- `:dao.stream/outcome` is present in every result map and is never repeated\n   107\t  in the per-outcome tables below; the keys those tables name are required in\n   108\t  addition to it.\n   109\t- Result maps are **open**: the contract fixes which keys are required per\n   110\t  outcome, and a consumer must ignore keys it does not understand. This is\n   111\t  what allows future extensions (for example readiness notification) to be\n   112\t  added without breaking existing consumers.\n   113\t- Exceptions are reserved for defects in the host's own assembly of\n   114\t  DaoStream, detected before any operation runs. Everything observable at an\n   115\t  operation returns data: closed, full, blocked, end, gap, not found,\n   120\t## The IO Model\n   121\t\n   122\tThree models are available for expressing IO. DaoStream is deliberately the\n   123\tthird.\n   124\t\n   125\t**Blocking (synchronous) IO.** The operation parks until it has an answer,\n   126\tthen returns it. Control flow stays sequential and the call site reads as\n   127\tordinary code, which is the whole of its appeal. The cost is that something\n   128\tmust wait — a thread per pending operation, held for its duration — and it\n   129\trequires a host that can park at all.\n   130\t\n   131\t**Callback (asynchronous) IO.** The operation registers a continuation and\n   132\treturns at once; the runtime invokes the continuation when the answer\n   133\tarrives. Nothing waits, so one thread carries many pending operations: this\n   134\tis the model that scales. Its cost is inverted control. The caller no longer\n   135\tdecides when it observes the answer, and the inversion is viral — on a host\n   136\twithout blocking, a promise cannot be absorbed inside a function, because\n   137\tawaiting returns to the caller at the first suspension point and makes the\n   138\tcaller's own result a promise in turn. Whatever is reached from a callback\n   139\tis reached from a callback.\n   140\t\n   141\t**Non-blocking polling.** The operation returns immediately with whatever is\n   142\ttrue now, including the answer \"nothing yet, ask again.\" Nothing parks and\n   143\tnothing is inverted: the caller keeps control and decides when to ask again.\n   144\tThis is POSIX `O_NONBLOCK` without `select`, and it is DaoStream's model.\n   145\t\n   146\t`next` is total and non-blocking (see Reading): every state of the stream\n   147\thas a defined data answer, and `:dao.stream/blocked` is `EAGAIN` — this\n   148\tposition holds nothing yet. `append!`'s `:dao.stream/full` is the same\n   149\tanswer for writes. No operation in this contract parks, and none takes a\n   150\tcallback; the Result Convention's \"never exceptions and never callbacks\" is\n   151\tthis model stated as a rule about results.\n   152\t\n   153\tThe rule generalizes past `next`: **no operation waits.** An operation that\n   154\tcannot yet know its answer returns the outcome true at the moment it is\n   155\tcalled, and the eventual answer arrives the way every other fact does — as\n   156\tdata, observed later, through an ordinary operation or on a stream. A\n   157\tDaoStream operation is never a request for something to happen and be\n   158\treported back. It is a question about now.\n   159\t\n   202\t## Creation and Attachment\n   203\t\n   204\tCreation brings a new logical stream into existence. Attachment joins an\n   205\texisting one. They are separate operations with separate inputs.\n   206\t\n   207\t- `create!` consumes a creation specification and returns a handle on a\n   208\t  new logical stream. `descriptor` on that handle returns `{ok}` with both\n   209\t  `:dao.stream/descriptor`, the transport-specific reachability value, and\n   210\t  `:dao.stream/identity`, the transport-independent logical-stream identity.\n   211\t  The descriptor carries the same identity internally; the sibling projection\n   212\t  and its value inside `:dao.stream/descriptor` must be structurally equal.\n   213\t  Both projections are total and outlive any attachment, so `descriptor` has\n   214\t  no failure outcome.\n   215\t- `attach!` consumes a portable descriptor and returns a handle on an\n   216\t  attachment to the **same** logical stream the descriptor names. Attaching\n   217\t  never creates.\n   218\t- A creation specification says how to build a stream. A descriptor only\n   219\t  names one — never executable code, never a construction program, never a\n   220\t  request to load a plugin.\n   221\t\n   222\tStreams are values that can be sent through streams — up to a serialization\n   223\tboundary. An in-memory stream whose admission declaration permits host\n   224\treferences can carry a handle intact. A dynamically delivered handle travels\n   225\tas composition data together with its declared surface, for example\n   226\t`{:dao.stream/handle h :dao.stream/surface #{:reader :writer}}`; the receiving\n   227\tinterpreter uses only that declared surface, and attaching to what it already\n   228\tholds is meaningless. A composition may omit the wrapper only when the\n   229\treceiver's fixed contract already declares the surface. But a handle does not\n   230\tsurvive encoding, and any\n   231\ttransport that encodes its elements — a file as much as a network socket —\n   232\tis a serialization boundary. Descriptors exist for crossing those\n   233\tboundaries, where the live value cannot travel and a name must. (What a\n   234\ttransport can carry is transport-owned: handing an encoding transport a\n   235\tvalue it cannot encode is reported as data like any other outcome.)\n   236\t`attach!` therefore consumes only descriptors, and\n   237\tresolves them against the transport's inherent operational state — the\n   238\tstreams a network endpoint serves because serving them is what it is, the\n   239\tfiles a filesystem holds. There is no stream table in this contract: no\n   240\tregistry maps descriptors to live handles, so a descriptor arriving\n   241\tsomewhere with nothing behind it — a ring buffer's descriptor on any other\n   242\thost, or on its own host absent a directory some composition chose to keep —\n   243\thonestly resolves to `:dao.stream/not-found`.\n   244\t\n   245\t### Envelopes\n   246\t\n   247\tA **creation specification** is a map with one required key:\n   248\t\n   249\t- `:dao.stream/type` — a qualified keyword naming the transport. This is the\n   250\t  dispatch key.\n   251\t\n   252\tEvery other key is transport-owned, qualified under the transport's namespace\n   253\t(for example a ring buffer's capacity), and validated by the selected handler\n   254\t— generic validation checks only the envelope, and a handler that rejects its\n   255\town keys returns `:dao.stream/invalid-spec`.\n   256\t\n   257\tThe **portable descriptor** is inspired by CORBA's interoperable object\n   258\treference. It is reachability data, not the logical-stream identity itself.\n   259\tIts properties are settled:\n   260\t\n   261\t- It carries `:dao.stream/type` — the transport, as above — as its dispatch\n   262\t  key.\n   263\t- It carries the logical-stream identity under the contract-owned\n   264\t  `:dao.stream/identity` key. Different descriptors may reach\n   265\t  the same logical stream through different endpoints; their reachability data\n   266\t  may differ, but their logical-stream identity is structurally equal. The same\n   267\t  descriptor attaches to the same stream every time.\n   268\t- It is self-contained. If the stream is remote, it contains whatever is\n   269\t  needed to connect — perhaps an IP address and port. The entry data is\n   270\t  transport-owned; a handler that cannot make sense of it returns\n   271\t  `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor`.\n   272\t- A transport whose attachments are distinguishable assigns each attachment\n   273\t  an opaque, structurally serializable value, unique among the attachments\n   274\t  that transport can tell apart. Every attachment handle the transport mints\n   275\t  has such an identity, whether the handle is returned by `attach!` or\n   276\t  minted from a host connection accepted without an `attach!` call. An\n   277\t  `attach!` success map carries the value under `:dao.stream/attachment`.\n   278\t  When no `attach!` call produces the handle, the transport specification\n   279\t  names the deposited event or other stream-native mechanism by which the\n   280\t  composition receives the identity. Wherever an answer or event for that\n   313\t|-------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|----------------------|\n   314\t| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A new logical stream exists, its identity settled at once. Implies not that every resource backing it has finished being acquired. | `:dao.stream/handle` |\n   315\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-spec`    | The creation specification is malformed.      | —                    |\n   316\t| `:dao.stream/not-found`       | No transport here matches `:dao.stream/type`. | —                    |\n   317\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The handler failed.                           | —                    |\n   318\t\n   319\t`attach!` outcomes:\n   320\t\n   321\t| Outcome                          | Meaning                                                                             | Required keys        |\n   322\t|----------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------|\n   323\t| `:dao.stream/ok`                 | A handle exists through which this attachment is operated. Implies neither that a far end has confirmed the stream it names nor that establishment has completed. Where either answer is one only the far end can give, it arrives later as data, on the channel the transport names (see Surfaces). | `:dao.stream/handle`, and `:dao.stream/attachment` where the transport distinguishes attachments |\n   324\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor` | The descriptor is malformed.                                                        | —                    |\n   325\t| `:dao.stream/not-found`          | The descriptor names no stream here, or no transport matches its type.              | —                    |\n   326\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error`    | The handler failed.                                                                 | —                    |\n   327\t\n   328\tWhere a transport acquires its medium through a process rather than at once —\n   329\tbinding a port, opening a handle through an API that only answers later — `ok`\n   330\tstill means the logical stream exists: its identity is minted, its sequence is\n   331\tempty, and the handle is operable. What it does not mean is that the stream can\n   332\tyet accept values. The handle reports that through its own operations, exactly\n   333\tas any other transient condition is reported, and the result of the acquisition\n   334\tarrives as data.\n   335\t\n   336\tThis is permitted only where logical existence is genuinely independent of the\n   337\tresource. A transport that cannot mint identity and lifecycle before its\n   338\tacquisition completes is not creating a stream and deferring its medium; it is\n   339\tdeferring the creation itself, and `ok` would be false. Such a transport does\n   340\tnot implement `create!` — what it needs is a request whose result arrives\n   341\tlater, which is an ordinary interpreter over streams, not an operation here.\n   342\t\n   343\tCreation defers differently from attachment, and the difference is why this is\n   344\ta caveat rather than a new outcome. `attach!` defers a **remote confirmation**:\n   345\tan answer only a far end can give, which is why it arrives as deposited data.\n   346\t`create!` has no far end. What it defers is **local acquisition**, whose\n   347\tfailure surfaces through the handle the caller already holds. A \"not yet\"\n   348\toutcome would have to return no handle — leaving the caller an identity it\n   349\tcannot operate, and nothing to retry against.\n   350\t\n   351\t### Host Dispatch\n   473\t## Cursors\n   474\t\n   475\tA cursor is an immutable value owned by the interpreter that holds it.\n   476\t\n   477\t- Cursors are minted by the stream: `cursor` on a handle takes an **anchor**\n   478\t  and returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/cursor`. The contract\n   479\t  defines two anchors — `:dao.stream/oldest`, the earliest retained\n   480\t  position, and `:dao.stream/newest`, positioned after the newest appended\n   481\t  value so the first `next` observes the next value to arrive. A transport\n   482\t  whose medium is naturally addressable may accept additional\n   483\t  transport-owned anchors (a byte offset, a timestamp). An anchor that is\n   484\t  malformed or not honored by this transport yields\n   485\t  `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`. On a handle whose attachment has closed,\n   486\t  `cursor` yields `:dao.stream/closed`; a handle on the logical stream\n   487\t  itself keeps minting cursors after close, so retained history remains\n   488\t  readable. Minting may have to consult the medium — locating the earliest\n   489\t  retained position on a durable log is itself a read — so where a\n   490\t  transport's reads can fail at runtime, minting can too, and `cursor`\n   491\t  yields `:dao.stream/transport-error` for it; a transport whose medium\n   492\t  cannot fail this way excludes the outcome like any other (see Surfaces).\n   493\t  `cursor`'s outcome set is therefore `:dao.stream/ok` (with\n   494\t  `:dao.stream/cursor`), `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`,\n   495\t  `:dao.stream/closed`, and `:dao.stream/transport-error`.\n   496\t- A composition that intends to observe events caused by an operation mints\n   497\t  its `:dao.stream/newest` cursor **before** invoking that operation. Minting\n   498\t  after `attach!`, endpoint bind, handoff installation, or any other operation\n   499\t  that can deposit would intentionally skip events deposited in between.\n   500\t- **A freshly minted `:dao.stream/oldest` is a position, not a claim about\n   501\t  completeness.** The anchor names the earliest *retained* position, which on\n   502\t  an evicting transport advances as values are lost. A `gap` is reported to a\n   503\t  cursor that spans an eviction; a cursor minted after one is a valid cursor\n   504\t  onto the surviving suffix and reports nothing, because nothing is wrong with\n   505\t  the cursor. Retained history is not complete history, and no anchor can make\n   506\t  it so. A consumer that requires complete history gets it from the transport's\n   507\t  declared retention (see *Retention and Gaps*), never from an anchor.\n   508\t- A composition on a transport that **can evict**, intending to detect whether\n   509\t  history was lost, mints an **origin cursor** — `:dao.stream/oldest` before\n   510\t  the first append — and keeps it, exactly as a composition that intends to\n   511\t  observe events caused by an operation mints `:dao.stream/newest` before\n   512\t  invoking it. A kept origin cursor converts a silent loss into a reported\n   513\t  `gap`. It cannot be minted afterwards and cannot be reconstructed: a consumer\n   514\t  that arrives later holds no evidence about what preceded it. On a transport\n   515\t  that declares complete retention this is unnecessary — a fresh `:oldest` is\n   516\t  the origin there, however late the consumer arrives.\n   517\t- Every valid cursor comes from the stream: minted by `cursor`, received as\n   518\t  a successor from `next`, or recovered from a gap outcome. Consumers never\n   519\t  construct cursor internals or fabricate positions — there is no `seek`. A\n   520\t  cursor's representation belongs to the transport.\n   521\t- A cursor binds to the logical stream, not to a handle: any handle on the\n   522\t  same logical stream accepts it, so a cursor handed to another interpreter\n   523\t  works with that interpreter's own handle. Whether a cursor survives\n   524\t  serialization to another host is transport-owned and TBD, like the\n   525\t  descriptor envelope.\n   526\t- A cursor carries the logical-stream identity, not a reachability descriptor.\n   527\t  `cursor-mismatch` compares that identity, so handles reached through distinct\n   528\t  descriptors for the same logical stream accept the same cursor.\n   529\t- Advancing is receiving a successor cursor from `next`. The stream never\n   530\t  advances a reader; the stream holds no reader positions at all. Dropping a\n   531\t  cursor is how a reader detaches — there is nothing to unregister.\n   532\t- Because cursors are immutable values, a reader may retain an old cursor and\n   533\t  re-read retained history, or hand a cursor to another interpreter. What a\n   534\t  cursor can still observe is governed only by retention.\n   535\t\n   536\t## Reading\n   537\t\n   538\t`next` takes a handle and a cursor and returns an outcome immediately.\n   539\tIt is total and non-blocking: every state of the stream has a defined data\n   540\tanswer.\n   541\t\n   542\t| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                       | Required keys                                                                           |\n   543\t|-------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n   544\t| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A value was observed.                                                         | `:dao.stream/value`, `:dao.stream/cursor` (successor)                                   |\n   545\t| `:dao.stream/blocked`         | Nothing at this position yet; retry later.                                    | —                                                                                       |\n   546\t| `:dao.stream/end`             | What this handle is on is closed and this position is past the retained tail. | —                                                                                       |\n   547\t| `:dao.stream/gap`             | This position was evicted.                                                    | `:dao.stream/cursor` (earliest retained position, or the tail when nothing is retained) |\n   548\t| `:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch` | The cursor was minted by a different logical stream.                          | —                                                                                       |\n   549\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-cursor`  | The value is not a cursor.                                                    | —                                                                                       |\n   550\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the read; nothing was observed.               | —                                                                                       |\n   551\t\n   552\tReading never mutates the stream. Two readers with cursors at the same\n   553\tposition observe the same retained value; neither can starve or affect the\n   558\t## Writing\n   559\t\n   560\t`append!` takes a handle and a value and returns an outcome:\n   561\t\n   562\t| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                                                                                              | Required keys |\n   563\t|-------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------|\n   564\t| `:dao.stream/ok`              | The value is accepted at the next position in the sequence this handle's writer surface is on. Implies neither local readability nor remote delivery. | —             |\n   565\t| `:dao.stream/full`            | The transport does not accept the value; nothing was appended. Whether this is transient or permanent is the transport's declared nature (see Retention and Gaps). | —             |\n   566\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-value`   | The transport cannot carry this value (for example, it cannot be encoded); nothing was appended.                                                     | —             |\n   567\t| `:dao.stream/closed`          | What this handle is on is closed to new appends; the value was not appended.                                                                         | —             |\n   568\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the append; nothing was appended.                                                                                    | —             |\n   569\t\n   570\tAcceptance is handle-relative, as close outcomes are. **Which sequence** a\n   571\thandle's writer surface is on is part of the transport's declared nature: for a\n   572\tring buffer it is the logical stream itself, so `ok` places the value in it; for\n   573\ta transport that carries values toward a stream elsewhere it is the ordered\n   574\toutbound path, and what becomes of the value at the far end is reported there,\n   575\tnot here. No remote transport can say more — under *no operation waits*, an\n   576\tanswer from the far end cannot arrive before `append!` returns.\n   577\t\n   578\tThe writer decides what to do about any non-`ok` outcome; DaoStream does not\n   579\tretry, buffer, or notify on its behalf.\n   921\t**Proposed resolution.** Two decisions, the first a choice:\n   922\t\n   923\t1. **Choose what a logical identity ranges over.** Either\n   924\t   (a) *one origin, one transport*: a logical stream is the sequence one\n   925\t   transport instance owns. A copy on another host or medium is a different\n   926\t   logical stream with its own identity; that it mirrors the first is a fact for an\n   927\t   interpreter to record. This is how the code already behaves:\n   928\t   `dao.stream.forward` copies into a destination with its own identity. The\n   929\t   sentence\n   930\t   about different descriptors reaching one stream narrows to \"different\n   931\t   endpoints of the same owning transport\". Or\n   932\t   (b) *contract-level position*: the contract defines a position within an\n   933\t   identity (an ordinal), cursors carry it under a `:dao.stream/…` key, and any\n   934\t   transport serving that identity honors it.\n   935\t   Recommended: (a). It matches Axiom 2 (sameness between sequences is an\n   936\t   interpretation), costs a paragraph, and leaves (b) available later as an\n   937\t   additive key. (b) commits every future transport, including ones with no\n   938\t   natural ordinal, now.\n   939\t2. **Settle serialization.** Replace \"TBD\" with: \"A cursor is plain data and\n   940\t   survives the host serialization codec structurally unchanged, under the same\n   941\t   rule as the envelopes. A cursor that outlives the stream it names yields\n   942\t   `not-found` at `attach!` or `cursor-mismatch` at `next`, never a defect.\"\n   943\t   Both existing transports already satisfy this.\n   944\t\n   945\t**Cost if deferred.** The first durable or replicated transport decides both\n   946\tquestions implicitly, by whatever its cursor happens to look like, and every\n   947\tconsumer that persisted a cursor inherits that accident.\n   948\t\n   949\t### OD-4. A logical stream can close but cannot end\n   950\t\n   951\t**The assumption.** *Complete history* obliges a transport to retain every\n   952\telement \"for as long as the logical stream exists\", and *Close* says close\n   953\t\"erases nothing\". Nothing says how a logical stream stops existing. The\n   954\tcontract assumes storage is either bounded by eviction or kept forever.\n   955\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '300,330p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '300,330p'\"","aggregated_output":"   300\tListed in §12. Additionally: a copy-serving mode remains useful (broadcast to many readers with local retention) and is what `dao.stream.serving` already is; it should be renamed or re-described so that \"serving\" no longer suggests attachment to the original stream (C2).\n   301\t\n   302\t---\n   303\t\n   304\t# Open questions for the owner\n   305\t\n   306\t- **Q1. Append through a proxy.** As designed, `append!` on a proxy answers `ok` for acceptance into the session's outbound path and the source's outcome arrives later on the event medium, as ws does and the contract's Writing section allows. The UCF's `:put` rule (\"retries its retained value and resumes on `ok`\") would then resume a program on an append the source may still refuse. Alternative: the proxy answers `full` until the source's outcome arrives and then reports that outcome, holding the value meanwhile, which gives the writer the source's truth from `append!` itself at the cost of a proxy-level convention (one held value, writer retries the same value). Recommendation: keep the contract-consistent design and amend UCF §7.4.3 so that a `:put` on a served stream resumes when the source's outcome is observed, not on outbound `ok`.\n   307\t- **Q2. Held reads in v1.** They cut network chatter for `blocked`-heavy readers such as a parked VM polling a response cell, at the cost of per-session server state and a hold budget. Recommendation: include, since the UCF's dominant pattern is exactly a kept response cursor polled until a value lands.\n   308\t- **Q3. Peer identity.** Opaque composition-assigned strings now, self-certifying kickoff-hash identities later, or self-certifying from the start. Recommendation: opaque now; the relay routes by string equality either way, and signature verification is an additive rule on `:serve/register`.\n   309\t- **Q4. Relay and rendezvous gating.** v1 accepts any `:serve/register` and `:serve/dial`. Recommendation: accept for the dev-only repo, record postage as the intended answer.\n   310\t- **Q5. Accept OD-1, OD-2 and OD-3 (a)+(2) now.** This design is not implementable without OD-3 and is dishonest without OD-2; OD-1 is needed only for the `:establishing` answer. Recommendation: accept all three as a precondition of the implementation phase.\n   311\t- **Q6. Lifetime of a served entry after migration.** Lease-governed (recommended, via `dao.lease`), or composition-retired with `not-found` as the honest late answer.\n   312\t- **Q7. Naming.** `dao.stream.serve` for this layer and a rename of today's `dao.stream.serving` to `dao.stream.serving.copy` or similar, so the two models are not confused.\n   313\t\n   314\t# Contradictions found\n   315\t\n   316\t| Severity | File:line | Evidence | Recommended correction |\n   317\t|---|---|---|---|\n   318\t| high | `docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:18-26` | \"serving is what makes any stream remotely attachable … remote interpreters can attach\" | Only true of copies: the client reads a local medium with local positions (OD-3 evidence, `dao.stream.md:906-911`). Reword to: the ws transport serves copies; attaching to the original stream is `dao.stream.serve`. |\n   319\t| high | `docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:9-16, 455-459` | descriptor \"names a server-hosted stream\"; endpoint constructor is bind-host/advertised-host only; no outbound-serving mode | Add: direction of establishment is independent of the serving role; a dialed-out attachment may carry `serve-step`. The state machine already permits it; only the prose forbids it. |\n   320\t| high | `docs/design/dao.stream.md:523-525` (OD-3), `:851-894` (OD-2), `:811-849` (OD-1) | \"Whether a cursor survives serialization to another host is transport-owned and TBD\"; append's third state undefined; unrecognized outcome undefined | This design requires OD-3 (a)+(2) and OD-2, and uses OD-1's `:dao.stream/retry?`. Accept them; move the text into the sections they amend. |\n   321\t| high | `docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:520-534` | \"standard stream-over-network facade derived from the dao.stream operation and outcome contract … `dao.stream.rpc.ws` demonstrates … does not itself supply the UCF facade\" | Cite `dao.stream.serve.md`; the facade is the serve protocol, the proxy is its handle, and the exporter's obligation is §9 of this design. |\n   322\t| medium | `docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:554-556` | \"a `:put` wait retries its retained value and resumes with it on `ok`\" | Through a proxy, `ok` is outbound acceptance. Resume on the observed source outcome (Q1), or adopt the alternative and say so. |\n   323\t| medium | `docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:780, 929` | `:yin.k/cursor-profiles #{:dao.stream/file-v1}` — a per-transport profile set | One profile, `:dao.stream.serve/v1` (cursor and outcomes in the portable domain). Per-transport profiles would make the resumer know transports, which the proxy exists to prevent. |\n   324\t| medium | `docs/design/daostream-udp-design.md:199-284, 470` | DRDS sequence numbers, ACK vectors, sliding window; \"NAT traversal (STUN / ICE)\" as deferred | Superseded: serve reads are idempotent by cursor, so no DRDS; a datagram stream with its own positions is a different logical stream under OD-3(a). Mark the document superseded prior art; NAT moves to this spec. |\n   325\t| medium | `docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:692-697` | Liveness and resumption protocol listed as deferred | Resumption is answered by kept cursors plus a stateless server (§5); liveness by `:serve/ping` at the serve layer (§7.6). Point the deferred items here. |\n   326\t| low | `docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:165-167` | \"NAT traversal is unaddressed … hole-punching, relay/TURN, and bootstrap discovery\" | Cite §7.3–7.4; a DHT node is a natural rendezvous host and rendezvous topics (`dao.stream.discovery.md` §4) are the bootstrap. |\n   327\t| low | `src/cljc/dao/stream/serving.cljc:1-11, 234-237` | \"Host-owned WebSocket serving composition\"; forwards from `:forward-anchor` (default oldest) into every accepted socket | Not wrong, but it is copy-serving. Rename or re-describe (Q7) so that \"serving\" does not imply attachment to the original stream. |\n   328\t| low | `docs/design/dao.lease.md:286-289` | \"A remote holder learns of a reclaim by observing … an ordinary `:ws/closed`. No close code is assigned here\" | With serve sessions, reclaim of a served entry is `:serve/close` with `:serve/reason`, independent of the channel's close codes; update once Q6 is decided. |\n   329\t\n   330\tThe task's read-only constraint was honored: no repository file was edited. The plan-mode plan file at `~/.claude/plans/read-collab-1790335900000-architect-dao-declarative-otter.md` records the context and verification items for this design.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
