{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md | sed -n '60,80p;145,168p;215,233p'\"","aggregated_output":"    60\t\n    61\tWhat DNS gets wrong, for our purposes, is the singular root: ICANN and the root\n    62\tsigning key are a governance chokepoint. Keep the sharding; dissolve the root.\n    63\t\n    64\tThe constraint to design under is **Zooko's triangle**: names can be decentralized,\n    65\tsecure, and human-meaningful — pick two. DNS picks secure + memorable and pays with\n    66\tthe central root. The mechanisms below each pick a different pair, and they compose.\n    67\t\n    68\t## The Mechanisms\n    69\t\n    70\t### 1. Self-certifying identities (secure + decentralized)\n    71\t\n    72\tThe kickoff hash *is* the name. A stream announces its signed head into the DHT under\n    73\tits identity hash; resolution is one lookup plus local signature verification against\n    74\tthe key the kickoff commits to. No authority exists because none is needed — the name\n    75\tproves its own binding (git commit hashes, IPFS CIDs). This is the foundation layer:\n    76\tevery other mechanism resolves *to* these names. Its defect is that a hash is not a\n    77\tname a human can hold.\n    78\t\n    79\tA directory entry binds an *identity*, not a state, so it never goes stale when a\n    80\tstream's head moves — directory churn stays at true membership churn.\n   145\t## Discovery as Attention, and Where Markets Belong\n   146\t\n   147\tDiscovery at internet scale is attention allocation: attention is the scarce\n   148\tresource, spam is its theft, Sybil flooding is counterfeit demand for it. Economics is\n   149\tthe right lens for adversarial scarcity — and the mechanisms above are weakest exactly\n   150\twhere pricing is strong: cold start (nobody follows you yet) and spam (announcing is\n   151\tfree). But \"attention market\" blurs three transactions that must be separated:\n   152\t\n   153\t- **Paying to be seen** — the speaker pays for reach. That is advertising,\n   154\t  definitionally. **Rejected.** It ranks streams by willingness-to-pay (i.e., by\n   155\t  capitalization, not relevance), and it contradicts the project's own pay-for-truth\n   156\t  thesis (`agent-web.md`): pay-for-truth flows money *toward* the party being attended\n   157\t  to, at the attendee's request; paid reach flows money *from* the party demanding\n   158\t  attention. Opposite signs. It is also born Goodharted (below).\n   159\t- **Paying to deposit** — a tiny flat cost to announce (Hashcash, postage).\n   160\t  **Accepted.** This prices spam without ranking by wealth. Note it needs a *cost*,\n   161\t  not a *market*: proof-of-work or burned stamps suffice; no exchange, no price\n   162\t  discovery, no ledger.\n   163\t- **Paying for discovery service** — buying an indexer's query answers.\n   164\t  **Accepted.** That is pay-for-truth applied to discovery, already the doctrine.\n   165\t\n   166\t### Goodhart's law\n   167\t\n   168\t\"When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure\" (Goodhart, 1975). A\n   215\t  own streams. A ranking is just another published, reader-verifiable view; competing\n   216\t  indexers with different seeds coexist like competing directories. (Google-the-company\n   217\t  is what you get when the link graph is proprietary reconstruction; here the graph is\n   218\t  a commons and ranking is a service anyone can render.)\n   219\t\n   220\t## Dependencies and Open Questions\n   221\t\n   222\tEverything above rests on prerequisites already tracked elsewhere:\n   223\t\n   224\t1. **Signed heads / kickoff-hash identity** (a stream-layer prerequisite, not\n   225\t   mutable DaoJing state; see `dao.jing.dht.md`) — mechanism (1) *is* this\n   226\t   discipline; (2)–(4) resolve to it.\n   227\t2. **Source-scoped query inputs** (`dao.space.query.md`; `datom.md`, d5:\n   228\t   SOURCE SCOPE) — sources stay separate database inputs and equal stream-local\n   229\t   ids unify only when the query deliberately joins them; prerequisite for\n   230\t   folding strangers' directories.\n   231\t3. **Postage design** — what a deposit costs and who verifies it (proof-of-work vs.\n   232\t   stamps) is unspecified.\n   233\t4. **Pool liveness** — explicit source pools need a refresh convention; the stigmergic\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '1202,1255p;1322,1340p;1445,1461p'\"","aggregated_output":"  1202\t**Lift** (emitter side; composition-driven, never automatic on every park):\n  1203\t\n  1204\t1. The machine is parked or blocked with a local record `r`, and its\n  1205\t   ready-queue is empty, else `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\n  1206\t2. **Enter exporting** (§7.7.4): detach the wait set into the export\n  1207\t   record, neutralize direct `:resume`. From here the source is fenced; no\n  1208\t   poll, retry, or local resume can perform an effect.\n  1209\t3. `lift(export-record)`: confirm the record is at a safepoint (§7.4.1),\n  1210\t   else `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; canonicalize every referenced segment and\n  1211\t   compute its address (§7.3.2), else the refusal; encode (§7.5), else the\n  1212\t   refusal; compute `:yin.k/requires` and `:yin.k/store` by the fixed point\n  1213\t   (§7.6), recording the discovery status; stamp the contract (§7.3.3); on\n  1214\t   first export, mint the occurrence; mint `:yin.k/id`.\n  1215\t4. Append the canonical instruction vectors of every required segment to a\n  1216\t   `dao.jing` intake pool (the payloads *are* the vectors, §7.3.4), then\n  1217\t   the id-less value body to the carrier medium; observe `:dao.stream/ok`\n  1218\t   on each. `:yin.k/carried` may then be empty. Retry of any failed append\n  1219\t   reuses the occurrence (§7.7.4).\n  1220\t5. Under `:exclusive`, append `:yin.k/offered` to the arbitration medium.\n  1221\t   The export record stays for recovery; the emitter is now a candidate\n  1222\t   like any other, and wakes only on custody (a grant to itself), never on\n  1223\t   data.\n  1224\t\n  1225\t**Lower** (resumer side):\n  1226\t\n  1227\t1. Read the value; check `:yin.k/contract`, else `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`.\n  1228\t2. Verify `:yin.k/id` by re-hashing the body, else `:yin.k/hash-mismatch`.\n  1229\t3. Decode against the §7.5.1 grammar with full structural validation, else\n  1230\t   `:yin.k/undecodable` — a correct hash is not structural validation, and\n  1231\t   both checks always run.\n  1232\t4. Evaluate `:yin.k/requires` (§7.6.5); fetch what may be fetched (segments\n  1233\t   by address, verified by re-canonicalization); else\n  1234\t   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with the discovery status.\n  1235\t5. Under `:exclusive`, attach to the arbitration medium (an attachment\n  1236\t   failure here is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming it — the value declares its\n  1237\t   authority, §7.7.3), propose, then observe the grant, else\n  1238\t   `:yin.k/not-holder` with the lease state observed; while a proposal is\n  1239\t   unanswered the driver's step answers `:yin.k/awaiting-grant` — a\n  1240\t   grantor owes no answer and no bound applies (`dao.lease.md`,\n  1241\t   *Authority*).\n  1242\t6. Load every required segment by address into the per-stamp index,\n  1243\t   verifying each (§7.3.4).\n  1244\t7. **Restore into an isolated store** (§7.6.2): the slice, the remapped\n  1245\t   cells, the resumer's FFI pair. Attach to every stream descriptor in the\n  1246\t   frame, pending waits, and cells (§7.4.3); an attachment `not-found` is\n  1247\t   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream. **Any failure from this step\n  1248\t   onward — after custody was acquired — releases first**: the resumer\n  1249\t   appends `:dao.lease/released`, the occurrence returns to offered, and\n  1250\t   the failure outcome is returned. A resumer that dies without releasing\n  1251\t   is recovered by the lease's own lapse; nothing else is owed.\n  1252\t8. `lower(frame, layout)` into engine registers; re-mint every pending wait\n  1253\t   in the local wait set; seed `:id-counter` per §7.6.3; run — through a\n  1254\t   fenced writer under `:exclusive` (§7.7.5).\n  1255\t9. At the next safepoint or halt, produce the successor occurrence\n  1322\t  its wiring becomes the worked example. A composition without a\n  1323\t  transactable space offers `:yin.k/fork` only.\n  1324\t- **Execution identity.** Occurrence minting, the `:yin.k/origin` chain,\n  1325\t  retry idempotence (one occurrence per park), and successor completion as\n  1326\t  a grantor ledger transition need conformance tests, including the crash\n  1327\t  windows of §7.7.5.\n  1328\t- **Pending-state completeness.** Every pending variant of §7.4.3 must\n  1329\t  round-trip and resume *elsewhere*: retained writes, sent and retained FFI\n  1330\t  calls with both endpoints and the kept response cursor, and cursor cells\n  1331\t  through the aliasing scenarios of `check-wait-set`. This depends on a\n  1332\t  generated stream-over-network facade and a **portable cursor profile**\n  1333\t  that preserves the source stream's kept cursor and `gap` outcomes. The\n  1334\t  M4 string-backed stream test below is the required cross-host proof.\n  1335\t- **Enforceable fencing.** Epoch-checked, atomic-with-commitment admission\n  1336\t  and durable op-id plus intent dedup at enrolled consumers are design\n  1337\t  items for `dao.space.transactor.md`. A consumer without a shared atomic\n  1338\t  boundary declares at-least-once or fail-stop behavior; it cannot claim\n  1339\t  exactly-once custody from the lease alone.\n  1340\t- **Canonical byte encoding.** `:yin.code/hash` and `:yin.k/id` are exactly\n  1445\t  static kind alone.\n  1446\t  Test both FFI states and each call effect. `lift(lower(frame))` must\n  1447\t  reproduce the canonical frame. An undeclared pc gives\n  1448\t  `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; queued work gives `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\n  1449\t  Insufficient pending evidence refuses before publication, as does a\n  1450\t  missing portable cursor profile. Halt yields a result, not a frame.\n  1451\t- Facade acceptance: Park a continuation holding a string-backed local\n  1452\t  `dao.stream` with a kept cursor, then migrate to another network node.\n  1453\t  The exporter mechanically serves that stream through the standard\n  1454\t  facade; the cell descriptor contains the facade endpoint and kept\n  1455\t  cursor, and the receiver binds a proxy `dao.stream` to the original\n  1456\t  logical stream. Reads resume at the kept position. Force retention\n  1457\t  loss and verify the proxy returns `:dao.stream/gap` with the source's\n  1458\t  successor cursor, without replay or silent skip. Repeat with a source\n  1459\t  that cannot honor the profile: lift refuses `:yin.k/unsatisfied`; an\n  1460\t  unreachable endpoint on lower is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream.\n  1461\t- Landing and order: M4 covers the early two-engine gate, the table,\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
